select id, episode_id, cms_item_id, chunk_index, content, token_estimate, speakers, start_timestamp, end_timestamp, podcast_index, episode_name, slug, web_url, guest_name, guest_title, categories, short_description, podcast_length
from jsonb_to_recordset($jsonpayload$
[{"id": "2722b77d-86a9-4edf-8a86-3a254a1ca726", "episode_id": "ee121690-42a3-4ed7-8bba-6415092b2a63", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482a", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Charlie Lozner (27:40): Yeah, so when I graduated from college, I kind of followed the extremely well beaten path to New York City and worked in finance for two years in New York. And then after two years in New York, it was right when the.com bubble kind of burst. So I actually, I kind of had one of those sit downs where it's like, door number one, you're going to cover oil and gas stocks door number two, you can take this severance package. And I was like, boy, that severance package looks like a good down payment on a pickup truck. So I bought a Tacoma, this was like 2001, bought a Tacoma, drove back and forth across the country and did some climbing in the north Cascades, did some climbing in Colorado, climbed from my first fourteener and saw somebody back country skiing and was like, I'm never walking down a mountain again. That looks amazing. (28:42): So that was pretty formative, but I couldn't quite figure out how to get into the outdoor industry then. So I did another two year stint working for a finance company in Boston, but the whole time just had my eyes on how do I get into the outdoor industry and specifically the ski industry. I really wanted to work in skis. And so I ended up working for CAR Who skis and line skis, car who is backcountry, backcountry telemark at and cross country skis, and then line skis, super well-known, more free ride and freestyle ski company in Vermont. And I think I was making a hundred dollars a week. I was sleeping on my friend's couch for a couple months. I was planning on just going back to business school. And then about 10 days before business school started, I approached the owners of Caru online and said, can I skip business school and just come work here full time? (29:39): And we were like, yeah, yeah, please do. And I was like, can you pay me more than a hundred dollars a week? Which they did, which was great. So that was the entry, and it was the escape from the Northeast Corridor. It was the escape from the finance track, and I am glad it happened. So worked for Kahu in line for three years in Vermont, and then those two brands were sold to K two sports out in Seattle for a variety of reasons. And so my wife and I think it was two weeks after we got married, packed up the car and drove out to Seattle and we thought we'd be there for two or three years, but turned into almost 10. So worked at K two for a while on all the outdoor brands, so back country ski brands, cross country ski brands, and then their snowshoe brands. (30:37): And then went over to outdoor research where I was the marketing director at Outdoor Research for about six years and helped 'em launch apparel, launch their ski collection of apparel, and then really helped with figuring out content marketing for, or we did some really cool stuff with Zach Giffin and the Tiny House tour and creating little web episodes and stuff and a short film about the tiny house tour that got almost a million views on YouTube completely organically before you had to really pay Google to get your videos out there and then helped. What was cool and kind of transformative for me is helping outdoor research launch e-commerce and launch their catalog business. (31:34): And I did a lot of work with Backbone Media in those days and ultimately just kind of fell in love with backbone's culture, with the leadership team at Backbone, really with everybody at Backbone. Every single person I worked with when I was at or over at Backbone was so smart, so nice, so much fun to be around. I just fell in love with the energy around the company and approached Penn and Nate and Greg and was just like, let's work together and what are you going to do? I have no idea. But we both kind of took a mutual leap of faith and my wife and two kids, and we all moved down to Carbondale and it's been amazing.", "token_estimate": 962, "speakers": ["Charlie Lozner"], "start_timestamp": "27:40", "end_timestamp": "27:40", "podcast_index": 8, "episode_name": "Ep 8: Charlie Lozner | Climate Change and Personal Responsibility in the Outdoor Industry | Backbone Medi‪a‬", "slug": "ep-8-charlie-lozner-climate-change-and-personal-responsibility-in-the-outdoor-industry-backbone-media", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-8-charlie-lozner-climate-change-and-personal-responsibility-in-the-outdoor-industry-backbone-media", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "For Charlie, the work that gives him the most satisfaction is work that combats the effects of climate change. The outdoor industry has made strides in lobbying for Mother Earth but there is more work to be done.", "podcast_length": "44:30"}, {"id": "63385bd4-4ada-4c96-bf65-df82f693ffe6", "episode_id": "ee121690-42a3-4ed7-8bba-6415092b2a63", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482a", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Cole Heilborn (32:22): So now that you're in a leadership position and you get to influence the culture of the team, how do you approach that? How do you create the culture that you want to create?\n\nCharlie Lozner (32:35): It's a good question. I mean, I think so much of it was in place, and I think it was, I'm the newest to the leadership team by far. So much of it was in place with just the tone that has been set by Penn, the founder, the co-founder by Nate, by Greg, by Ian, and then just the veterans that have been around backbone for a really long time, Lindsey Brown, John Qualo, Corey Lowe, they've all been there for much, much longer than me. So the culture's in place, so some of it's like don't screw it up, (33:13): But I think it's a constant reminder. There's values that we hold and we are definitely a values led organization, and it's a reminder of circling back to those things that those are the most important things. We're not an organization that's going to undercut each other. We're the opposite. We're going to support each other and then just how we treat clients. And one of the things I took away from outdoor research, outdoor research when I was there was owned by Dan Nordstrom, part of the Nordstrom family, Nordstrom. There's been many books written about Nordstrom and the servant leader mentality, and that's something maybe I didn't get quite right at or I really didn't. I still had a very east coast style, but I made sure that when it came to backbone, I brought that servant leader mentality and just how can I be a better teacher? How can I support the team, whether that's somebody that's in their first couple months at the job or somebody that's been there forever.\n\nCole Heilborn (34:28): And in a small way, it's kind of how you leave your print on the people around you and the things that you touch. So it's super important, I think. Alright, last question for you. What is your craziest adventure story?", "token_estimate": 490, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Charlie Lozner"], "start_timestamp": "32:22", "end_timestamp": "34:28", "podcast_index": 8, "episode_name": "Ep 8: Charlie Lozner | Climate Change and Personal Responsibility in the Outdoor Industry | Backbone Medi‪a‬", "slug": "ep-8-charlie-lozner-climate-change-and-personal-responsibility-in-the-outdoor-industry-backbone-media", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-8-charlie-lozner-climate-change-and-personal-responsibility-in-the-outdoor-industry-backbone-media", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "For Charlie, the work that gives him the most satisfaction is work that combats the effects of climate change. The outdoor industry has made strides in lobbying for Mother Earth but there is more work to be done.", "podcast_length": "44:30"}, {"id": "c6303a63-9a70-46f6-b2ce-8f637926a7e9", "episode_id": "ee121690-42a3-4ed7-8bba-6415092b2a63", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482a", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Charlie Lozner (34:46): Yeah, I've actually got a pretty good one in the outdoor space. I mean, it's certainly not crazy, but it's just a fun adventure. So when I was at K two, I planned to go ski the F finger, which is big long line off Rainier. If you catch it in good conditions, it's the longest, I think it's the most vertical fall line run in North America. It's like 10,000 feet from the summit of Rainier down to the Quale Glacier and the Quale Bridge. And it looked like conditions. This was a while ago, so there wasn't quite as much just whatever great weather data at your fingertips and everything, but it looked like conditions were going to be great. So we, two friends from K two and I, we packed up, we drove down, it's a solid drive down to the paradise side of Rainier, and literally the last mile as we're pulling up to paradise, we start seeing that there's a bunch of new snow on the ground and it's like, oh, that's not good. (35:55): We've got 10,000 feet of exposure above us. So we pull into the ranger station, open the car door, and we're like, this is not happening. And we had all our gear and everything and there's no internet connection up there, at least there wasn't at the time. So we're just like, well, what do we do now? We've taken the day off of work tomorrow. I don't know what time it was, maybe three o'clock in the afternoon or four o'clock. So then we turn around, but we go down, we finally get cell service and start looking at the weather forecast and there might be where is it not snowed? So then we turn around and we drive back to Seattle. We get crampons and ice tools, or I guess we had some of that stuff, but we had to reload a bit different gear. Then we drive over the cascades to the base of Mount Stewart and we were going to, at this point, it's like midnight, but we still have time. (36:56): And so then we pull into basically the trail head at Mount Stewart. We just pull sleeping bags out, sleep underneath or next to the car, get up two hours later and head in to go basically climb and ski Mount Stewart. On what side would that be? Kind of the north, yeah, north side. I can't remember what it's called, the glacier on that side, but we head in just postholing. It hadn't quite frozen overnight. And eventually we start heading up and realize this is pre good GPS and stuff. You had to have good navigation skills and apparently we lacked those because we headed up the wrong drainage and just these micro drainages headed up the wrong drainage and just kept going and going and going and going. Finally hit snow, but not quite enough snow to put skis on and skin. So we just keep heading up. Finally realized that we were totally in the wrong place, and I think we all put our skis on. We made four turns on the worst snow possible, but we made four turns, then pretty much had to put our skis back on, our backs headed back out. And it was, I mean from when we left Seattle to when we got back to Seattle was an absolute epic and we spent probably eight to 12 hours suffering trying to get up the wrong drainage. (38:36): But yeah, so that was a crazy adventure because it was the most time I think I've spent in the outdoors for the least reward possible.", "token_estimate": 807, "speakers": ["Charlie Lozner"], "start_timestamp": "34:46", "end_timestamp": "34:46", "podcast_index": 8, "episode_name": "Ep 8: Charlie Lozner | Climate Change and Personal Responsibility in the Outdoor Industry | Backbone Medi‪a‬", "slug": "ep-8-charlie-lozner-climate-change-and-personal-responsibility-in-the-outdoor-industry-backbone-media", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-8-charlie-lozner-climate-change-and-personal-responsibility-in-the-outdoor-industry-backbone-media", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "For Charlie, the work that gives him the most satisfaction is work that combats the effects of climate change. The outdoor industry has made strides in lobbying for Mother Earth but there is more work to be done.", "podcast_length": "44:30"}, {"id": "8b36d86b-2a30-4a3a-b2c7-4aec06342a25", "episode_id": "ee121690-42a3-4ed7-8bba-6415092b2a63", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482a", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Cole Heilborn (38:48): Oh man. Oh, that's pretty good. But you got to have a few bad stories to make the good ones even better, so\n\nCharlie Lozner (38:54): Totally.\n\nCole Heilborn (38:54): Yeah,\n\nCharlie Lozner (38:56): That was definitely the worst sort of craziest, but\n\nCole Heilborn (38:59): Yeah. Awesome. Well, if people want to find you, if they want to find backbone and follow along with the great work you guys are doing, where can people find you guys?\n\nCharlie Lozner (39:09): So yeah, we're at backbone media.net. We might have the last.net URL out there. But yeah, we're at backbone media.net. You can find us certainly on Instagram. We've got a great content being pushed out there. We're doing more and more trend pieces that we're pushing out on LinkedIn, so you can check those out. A lot of stuff that we're seeing, just sort of trends we're seeing as COVID continues and how brands are evolving. But yeah, you can find us on our website, you can find us on Instagram, you can find us on LinkedIn. And I'm not a big social media person myself. I spend too much time buying ads from Facebook and Instagram that I don't really want to do that in my free time. But yeah, you can find me on the backbone website as well.\n\nCole Heilborn (39:59): Awesome. Sounds good. Well, thank you so much for your time. It's been great chatting. I hope you have a great weekend.\n\nCharlie Lozner (40:04): Thanks, you too. Get out and have some fun this weekend.\n\nCole Heilborn (40:07): Absolutely all. Take care.\n\nCharlie Lozner (40:08): See you.\n\nCole Heilborn (40:09): Thank you for listening to Brand Story. If you enjoyed this episode, I encourage you to share it with a friend or family member who also might enjoy, and please tune in next time for the next episode. Thank you.", "token_estimate": 446, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Charlie Lozner"], "start_timestamp": "38:48", "end_timestamp": "40:09", "podcast_index": 8, "episode_name": "Ep 8: Charlie Lozner | Climate Change and Personal Responsibility in the Outdoor Industry | Backbone Medi‪a‬", "slug": "ep-8-charlie-lozner-climate-change-and-personal-responsibility-in-the-outdoor-industry-backbone-media", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-8-charlie-lozner-climate-change-and-personal-responsibility-in-the-outdoor-industry-backbone-media", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "For Charlie, the work that gives him the most satisfaction is work that combats the effects of climate change. The outdoor industry has made strides in lobbying for Mother Earth but there is more work to be done.", "podcast_length": "44:30"}, {"id": "57078508-04ac-43a8-81e0-88d2b74b97a3", "episode_id": "d91752cb-569f-4180-97ae-74ffb79fc9b8", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4883", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Liz Esche (00:02): Absolutely. I mean, you should not go through a rebrand if you're just sick of your logo or leadership changes and you want to do something new and different. I mean, but we had a lot of really compelling reasons and I'm very excited about how everything's turned out and is turning out. We're still in process, still marketing through change, but we're starting to really see the benefits of those changes.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:30): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing podcast. My name is Cole Holborn and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Port Sided Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:02): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Hilborn. Today I'm sitting down with Liz Eshi. She's the marketing director from Gnarly Nutrition. Liz, how are you?\n\nLiz Esche (01:11): I'm doing great. Excited. Excited to be here.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:15): Thank you. Yeah, thanks for taking the time. This is the first episode that we've recorded in 2022, so we're first week here of January, and stoked to kick off the new year with you.\n\nLiz Esche (01:28): No pressure. Got to give me good then.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:33): No, it'll be great. How was your New Year's and Christmas?", "token_estimate": 410, "speakers": ["Liz Esche", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:02", "end_timestamp": "01:33", "podcast_index": 90, "episode_name": "Ep 90: Liz Esche | Marketing Through a Re-Brand | Gnarly Nutrition", "slug": "ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Liz Esche", "guest_title": "Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Liz Esche, the Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition joins me to discuss the re-brand project Gnarly rolled out.", "podcast_length": "39:50"}, {"id": "67d12838-a99a-41be-b090-08894c82356b", "episode_id": "d91752cb-569f-4180-97ae-74ffb79fc9b8", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4883", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Liz Esche (01:37): It was really good. Really great to see family. I was one of the lucky ones that took my COVID test, didn't have a positive and was able to see everyone. Some of my cousins were not so lucky, which was unfortunate. And then we did recently, I'm in the Denver area, so I am not too far from the Boulder County Fire, so just putting a heads up out there about that. There are people in need of support, coats, jackets, lots of livelihoods lost. So don't mean to start out on a negative, but if I have a platform, then I'm going to ask for a little support in that direction.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:18): By all means. Do you have any programs or groups that you'd push people towards if they have some extra things they can contribute?\n\nLiz Esche (02:25): Oh my goodness. There are a bunch in, actually Base Camp Outdoor has started a thread if you're in that Facebook group. So I'd point you in that direction. And then I know Deuter in Longmont has been collecting for the community as well.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:40): Awesome. Well, Liz, I don't mean to spring this on you, but we're going to try a new thing out here on the podcast. So I've gotten feedback from folks that usually when each episode we just jump right in full swing into our topic and I've received some feedback that people would like to learn a little bit more about the individual, the guest to you before we jump in full swing. So I've got a few quick fire questions that I'm going to throw your way and just to kind of set the tone.\n\nLiz Esche (03:10): Okay, let's do it.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:11): Alright, are you first questions? We just came out of the new year. Are you a resolution person or not?\n\nLiz Esche (03:18): I am a resolution person.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:20): Okay, great. Do you prefer snow or dirt?\n\nLiz Esche (03:22): Snow?\n\nCole Heilborn (03:24): If you're stuck in a tent and you only had to choose between eating ramen or tortillas, what would you choose?\n\nLiz Esche (03:31): Ramen.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:33): Okay. And last question, what's your favorite activity besides something outdoors?", "token_estimate": 523, "speakers": ["Liz Esche", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "01:37", "end_timestamp": "03:33", "podcast_index": 90, "episode_name": "Ep 90: Liz Esche | Marketing Through a Re-Brand | Gnarly Nutrition", "slug": "ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Liz Esche", "guest_title": "Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Liz Esche, the Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition joins me to discuss the re-brand project Gnarly rolled out.", "podcast_length": "39:50"}, {"id": "76cdb954-bf1a-4a21-8408-e902ec79ca28", "episode_id": "d91752cb-569f-4180-97ae-74ffb79fc9b8", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4883", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Liz Esche (03:39): Oh, so I'm a super reader and a writer on the side, and so I love reading big sci-fi nut. And I also like to write, not that I've been published anywhere important, but I do do it on the side.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:57): Oh, that's awesome. Sci-fi, that's a real good category. So like Star Wars, star Trek, that type of sci-fi or\n\nLiz Esche (04:02): Waiting in panicked anticipation for Dune to come out on film because I've loved that book since I was a child. That level.\n\nCole Heilborn (04:12): Right on. So as we discussed, you're the marketing director for Gnarly Nutrition. It sounds like you've come onto the role semi recently. You've been there for a bit, but tell us a little bit about how you got connected with Gnarly and what you've been up to.\n\nLiz Esche (04:27): Yeah, it's kind of a great story and essentially how this industry works. I was previously at Deuter and VO and a mountain bike company, gorilla Gravity. And on the side, as I said, I love books, I really like film. I was doing some work with Kathy Carlo at No Man's Land Film Festival, so I'm the program director there and once a year we get together and put this giant thing on. And Gnarly Nutrition was actually a sponsor of the film festival. So when they started looking for a marketing director, I kind of got connected and I knew already that they had a really great culture and Eli, the founder, was just an awesome human being and there's nothing more exciting than looking at a job opportunity and feeling really good about it and knowing you already have a lot of details. So I reached out and got connected through Kathy as well. And here we are and it's been almost a year getting 10, 11 months. We're getting close to that year mark, and it's just been an incredible opportunity and so much fun and exactly what I imagined it would be maybe a little harder at times because of how much we're changing, but it's been such a fun process and I have a great team around.", "token_estimate": 494, "speakers": ["Liz Esche", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "03:39", "end_timestamp": "04:27", "podcast_index": 90, "episode_name": "Ep 90: Liz Esche | Marketing Through a Re-Brand | Gnarly Nutrition", "slug": "ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Liz Esche", "guest_title": "Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Liz Esche, the Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition joins me to discuss the re-brand project Gnarly rolled out.", "podcast_length": "39:50"}, {"id": "3f9b06f7-dac3-4c74-8d67-d25923acc842", "episode_id": "d91752cb-569f-4180-97ae-74ffb79fc9b8", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4883", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Cole Heilborn (05:57): Eli was on the show, I think he was episode like 45, so almost probably more than a year ago. I think he was in your position. And so it's been great to chat with him and then connect with you and I think just see how much the company has changed and grown and really these last 10 or 11 months that you've been with them. We're going to kind of get into a little bit about what you guys have been up to and some of the challenges that you've been working through. But gnarly recently went through a pretty big rebrand and is still kind of actively rolling out some of the deliverables of that rebrand. Can you tell us a little bit about why did you guys embark down this path?", "token_estimate": 175, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "05:57", "end_timestamp": "05:57", "podcast_index": 90, "episode_name": "Ep 90: Liz Esche | Marketing Through a Re-Brand | Gnarly Nutrition", "slug": "ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Liz Esche", "guest_title": "Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Liz Esche, the Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition joins me to discuss the re-brand project Gnarly rolled out.", "podcast_length": "39:50"}, {"id": "f8dcd3cd-2fb8-4b32-83d8-56d04ee73ef3", "episode_id": "d91752cb-569f-4180-97ae-74ffb79fc9b8", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4883", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Liz Esche (06:40): Why? That is a great question and I actually gave a presentation earlier this year with some other folks in the outdoor industry about when is the right time to rebrand? And it all comes back to the why are you doing this for the right reasons? And we had really strong reasons, which was we felt like our branding wasn't communicating to our key audience. So we cater to the outdoor industry, we work with outdoor athletes, with climbers, with runners, with bikers, and our branding was a little bit more reminiscent of a just in the gym kind of nutrition brand. So we were talking to a different people than our branding seemed to be talking to, which was a bit confusing. We also wanted to be more playful and we evaluated our values and realized that a lot of nutrition companies seem to be talking about being the biggest in the Buffett or being the best person at X, Y, and Z. (07:46): And that's not the conversation we wanted to have. We want to talk about individual efforts, we want to talk about what we changed our motto to was push your possible, really those individual goals versus just being the most extreme and the absolute best. Everyone has a journey that they can go through and that was key as well. And then the final reason was we were going through a period of really evaluating our sustainability. It's always been important to us. We've always tried to source really well and have the most recyclable versions and we just could not get past these plastic tubs, which are a staple in the supplement industry. And then Eli and our CPO, Shannon O'Grady, she's brilliant, found this amazing solution and figured out a plan on how we were going to switch to steel tins. So there was also this opportunity, if we're totally switching to these steel tins, we're going to rebrand, we're going to come out with a new look, and on the other side it's going to feel like exactly who we are and who we have been and just be a better visual presentation of that and in a much more recyclable, better for the planet package. (09:08): So that was what I walked into day one.", "token_estimate": 527, "speakers": ["Liz Esche"], "start_timestamp": "06:40", "end_timestamp": "06:40", "podcast_index": 90, "episode_name": "Ep 90: Liz Esche | Marketing Through a Re-Brand | Gnarly Nutrition", "slug": "ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Liz Esche", "guest_title": "Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Liz Esche, the Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition joins me to discuss the re-brand project Gnarly rolled out.", "podcast_length": "39:50"}, {"id": "01c2479a-d1ea-4502-82fa-2f36d4ae4980", "episode_id": "d91752cb-569f-4180-97ae-74ffb79fc9b8", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4883", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Cole Heilborn (09:14): You mentioned you gave this presentation on when is the right time to rebrand and it sounds like for gnarly there's a bunch of components that were all kind of primed and were underway, and so it just felt like a natural time to turn to the next chapter.\n\nLiz Esche (09:30): Absolutely. I mean, you should not go through a rebrand if you're just sick of your logo or leadership changes and you want to do something new and different. But we had a lot of really compelling reasons and I'm very excited about how everything's turned out and is turning out. We're still in process, still marketing through change, but we're starting to really see the benefits of those changes.\n\nCole Heilborn (09:58): Yeah. Well you just alluded to our focus or topic for today's episode, this idea of marketing through change. I'm sure a lot of folks have been in similar situations. Rebrands don't come along that often. Ideally. Ideally you get it done with and then you're good for however many years, but when it's happening you find yourself in this really unique position where there's all these new things being developed, new looks, new deliverables, new assets, but you still have to keep the day-to-day running. You still have to keep the current operations running. So we're going to get a little bit into some of the weeds of how Liz, you kind of kept everything going while developing this entirely new brand. Could you kind of set the stage for us though, and describe though what were some of the deliverables that you guys were looking to refresh with this rebrand so folks have an idea?\n\nLiz Esche (10:48): Yeah, so everything changed, logo changed was updated, colors changed, motto changed. We reevaluated our values. Our website is completely migrating to a new platform because why not do that at the same time? We had to definitely look at all of our assets, all of our content and start building again from scratch. That doesn't mean you throw out all of our great content from the past. It just means that, alright, we're going to need these new assets, new labels on everything. It seems never ending, but it was a full restart.", "token_estimate": 540, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Liz Esche"], "start_timestamp": "09:14", "end_timestamp": "10:48", "podcast_index": 90, "episode_name": "Ep 90: Liz Esche | Marketing Through a Re-Brand | Gnarly Nutrition", "slug": "ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Liz Esche", "guest_title": "Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Liz Esche, the Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition joins me to discuss the re-brand project Gnarly rolled out.", "podcast_length": "39:50"}, {"id": "a6616477-6625-4af7-8bc2-ce058080c51b", "episode_id": "d91752cb-569f-4180-97ae-74ffb79fc9b8", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4883", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Cole Heilborn (11:31): Gotcha. So how'd you begin to approach this because you can't just shut things off and say, I'm only going to focus on the rebrand. You have to keep things moving forwards while simultaneously developing everything new. So what was your mentality going into it?\n\nLiz Esche (11:46): Well, it started with a deep breath. So I walked in Gnarly's out of Salt Lake City. So I flew in to meet the team and we kind of sat down and looked at everything that needed to change and it became apparent very quickly that this was not, we're not going to wake up one morning and have it all be new and fresh. That's just not, maybe you can do that as a larger company. Maybe you can do that as a smaller company. And it's just the mid-size companies that really struggle. I'm not sure, but we could not align our inventory with that process. We could not align the creation of our creative assets with that process and we definitely couldn't align our web migration. So then it became a phased approach and we looked at, okay, well what can we do right now and how do we make this clear for our customers and explain what we're doing and why? (12:44): And that was really important to us. We didn't want to try and hide what we were doing and keep it under the radar because at some point you're going to have different logos on different product and you're going to have a freshened up website with old labeled product being displayed and we couldn't get around that. So it really began with what's the timing and how do we release information to our customers? So we started very basic. We started with the logo and the colors. We had the rest of the branding figured out, but that's what we started with. And we sent out communications to our athletes who actually helped us spread the message and to our customers saying, this is step one of many and here's why we're doing it. We're doing it because we found better packaging because we want to connect better with you and this is truly embodying our spirit, this gnarly spirit. So being really transparent I think helped and just sending that constant communication, not an overwhelming amount, but acknowledging that things are happening was really key.", "token_estimate": 555, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Liz Esche"], "start_timestamp": "11:31", "end_timestamp": "11:46", "podcast_index": 90, "episode_name": "Ep 90: Liz Esche | Marketing Through a Re-Brand | Gnarly Nutrition", "slug": "ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Liz Esche", "guest_title": "Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Liz Esche, the Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition joins me to discuss the re-brand project Gnarly rolled out.", "podcast_length": "39:50"}, {"id": "37e60b90-0148-4446-89c4-c23cc52c3c7e", "episode_id": "d91752cb-569f-4180-97ae-74ffb79fc9b8", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4883", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Cole Heilborn (13:57): Gotcha. You mentioned something interesting. So you guys rolled everything out in different phases because you couldn't do it all in one go. Would you have preferred to have done it in one swoop one day someone wakes up and everything is new or was this trickle approach, how did that work out? I\n\nLiz Esche (14:17): Don't know. I think that's the dream, isn't it? To wake up one day and have everything be new. And in previous companies that I've worked for that are really dependent on seasonality, you can almost do that because you can set it for your spring launch and you can do that, but we don't work that way. We're not that big. We order inventory X amount of months out and we are keeping up with a kind of constant schedule. We have some high, but it's really pretty constant. So there was no clear stopping point based on our inventory where we were like, you know what? We're fine if we just throw out that amount of inventory that was never going to be okay with us or our values. So maybe it's the dream to launch all at once, but it's also opened the door to have this continued conversation with our customers and our long-term customers. I mean, we have people that have been using our product for six, eight years to explain why we're doing this and to keep them in the loop and give them previews and little tidbits of what's coming next. So overall, I'm really happy that we did it this way because it allowed us to have a longer, more in depth conversation with our audience and gave us a lot of little moments to highlight along the way. Whereas if we had done it in one fell swoop, how long can you really keep that exciting?\n\nCole Heilborn (15:52): How did you communicate with your customers through all of this? That sounds like an interesting component and maybe people don't think about when they think about a rebrand is like how do you communicate with your audience through all of it?", "token_estimate": 485, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Liz Esche"], "start_timestamp": "13:57", "end_timestamp": "15:52", "podcast_index": 90, "episode_name": "Ep 90: Liz Esche | Marketing Through a Re-Brand | Gnarly Nutrition", "slug": "ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Liz Esche", "guest_title": "Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Liz Esche, the Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition joins me to discuss the re-brand project Gnarly rolled out.", "podcast_length": "39:50"}, {"id": "350ca196-56e5-44f3-b65e-2e1eb851e9e1", "episode_id": "d91752cb-569f-4180-97ae-74ffb79fc9b8", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4883", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Liz Esche (16:04): So a lot of emails, I mean emails going to be the go-to. We definitely post it on our social channels and we asked very routinely for feedback and we're continuing to ask for feedback. We want our customers to be engaged, especially with our far more recyclable tin cans, we want our customers to give that feedback and show their excitement. So we've actually had a really high number of reviews coming in asking, can we put all of our product in these cans and you still have some stuff in bags, why don't we just move it all over? And they, they're really excited. And so it is just generally been emails and asking for that feedback and checking in. We definitely updated a lot of our flows at the post-purchase level to remind people, especially when we had, there were points where we had double products. So you might order a vegan protein and you may get it in the old label or you may get it in the new steel can. So a lot of post-purchase flows saying, Hey, this might be happening and here's why, and we're really excited about this and it's a bit awkward. We know it, but we're happy that you're here with us on this journey to something better. And everyone was on board. I wish I could say you just got to push through the detractors, but we didn't really have to detractors everyone, and that's kind of our target audience. So it made sense. Our audience are outdoors folks, so they're going to care about reducing the amount of plastic out there.\n\nCole Heilborn (17:48): Yeah, yeah, that's great to hear that it went fairly smoothly from that perspective. I just had an idea how you look back at some of the legacy outdoor brands now and you look at some of their old products with the old logo on it and they've become collectors items and you can spend way too much money on these old things if, because now I'm like, well, every rebrand that a company does in 50 years, that old product could be some heritage item. I wonder if there's a way to still create that collectible, instigate that collectible spirit with where you guys are at right now.", "token_estimate": 522, "speakers": ["Liz Esche", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "16:04", "end_timestamp": "17:48", "podcast_index": 90, "episode_name": "Ep 90: Liz Esche | Marketing Through a Re-Brand | Gnarly Nutrition", "slug": "ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Liz Esche", "guest_title": "Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Liz Esche, the Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition joins me to discuss the re-brand project Gnarly rolled out.", "podcast_length": "39:50"}, {"id": "ae66a750-92e9-412e-86e6-d6a98880e45e", "episode_id": "d91752cb-569f-4180-97ae-74ffb79fc9b8", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4883", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Liz Esche (18:25): Oh yeah. There's some products we will never give up. We had a very popular slogan, gnarly till death was one of our popular swag items. We have it on some hats and some t-shirts and I don't think those are going to be phased out. They're just way too popular. But maybe we will face them out and bring them back for special occasions. That's not a bad idea.\n\nCole Heilborn (18:51): Yeah. Well, any royalties you collect, you can send them my way. So tell me a little bit more about the switch from plastic to tin. That sounds like a really, sounds like an easy thing. Sounds like though it was not an easy thing. What was that process like?\n\nLiz Esche (19:09): I have to give credit to our CPO Shannon because every week she'd give us updates and just kind of push through the haze. It was a really difficult time to switch all of our packaging when supply chains were in chaos and still are in chaos. I think they're estimating chaos until 2023, who knows? So we knew it was going to be a process, but it had been a really long time coming. We wanted to switch to the steel cans and we knew we were almost going to have to do it product by product. We bought as much as we possibly could and then waited for current inventory to run out. And sometimes that timed well, we just got the new product in other times product ran out and we had a gap of three weeks and we had to go to our customers and say, I'm really sorry we were out. (20:13): That took a lot of foresight on Shannon's end and on our team's end to say, well, okay, we need to set aside X amount of product for our subscribers and for our current customers who we know are going to want this, and it's just going to be those new customers that have to wait, but it'll be worth it. So that was quite the process. We are down to our last, we're waiting on one more flavor in BCAAs to switch over, and then we are completely over to the new steel tins in all of our Tubbed products. So it's been a long journey. It started six months ago and it started seeing those changes and we're finally here.\n\nCole Heilborn (20:59): Wow, that's awesome. Well, congratulations. That's got to feel really good.", "token_estimate": 545, "speakers": ["Liz Esche", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "18:25", "end_timestamp": "20:59", "podcast_index": 90, "episode_name": "Ep 90: Liz Esche | Marketing Through a Re-Brand | Gnarly Nutrition", "slug": "ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Liz Esche", "guest_title": "Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Liz Esche, the Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition joins me to discuss the re-brand project Gnarly rolled out.", "podcast_length": "39:50"}, {"id": "895118e4-56c9-425d-b3a9-38e89bf071c9", "episode_id": "d91752cb-569f-4180-97ae-74ffb79fc9b8", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4883", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Liz Esche (21:02): It does feel really good. And even though it didn't always look the prettiest from a marketing from a creative direction standpoint, there were some really awkward moments, but the value behind it is really great and I think that's resonating with our customers.\n\nCole Heilborn (21:23): Well, and yeah, and it sounds like even though some parts were messy, people were on board with the reason why, and that committee comes back to why knowing the why is so important because it gives you your audience reasons to stick along through some of the bumps in the road as the company goes through the transition, right?\n\nLiz Esche (21:43): Yeah, exactly. I mean, we had to raise our prices to one to $2 across the line per product in order to support this new packaging. And sometimes as a marketer you have to be the bearer of bad news when things aren't going great, but they're going great, but raising prices is never a fun conversation. And we addressed that head on and just sent out a bunch of notifications that this was happening and really no one said they either didn't say anything or they said, don't care. It's one to $2 to get plastic out. So that was nice.\n\nCole Heilborn (22:27): Yeah, interesting. What was the moment, was there a moment, like a crux moment in the last year or so that was the hardest piece for you guys to overcome? What was that?", "token_estimate": 345, "speakers": ["Liz Esche", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "21:02", "end_timestamp": "22:27", "podcast_index": 90, "episode_name": "Ep 90: Liz Esche | Marketing Through a Re-Brand | Gnarly Nutrition", "slug": "ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Liz Esche", "guest_title": "Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Liz Esche, the Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition joins me to discuss the re-brand project Gnarly rolled out.", "podcast_length": "39:50"}, {"id": "0ef96034-71a7-412c-a065-b50ac7478939", "episode_id": "d91752cb-569f-4180-97ae-74ffb79fc9b8", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4883", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Liz Esche (22:39): The crux moment? We had some moments of really low inventory just waiting on new cans, and I guess that's kind of a scary prospect for a marketer is how do I continue marketing when I running out of product when we're still waiting because of everything that's going on? Because steel is suddenly three times more expensive and people are snapping it up as quickly as they can. How do I market when we're out of our top sellers? So that was something we leaned on consistently through all of this change is, well, what are we good at as a brand? And that's our education. So we just leaned more deeply into our education, into our clinics, into our content. I have an incredible content manager on my team, Tim biac, who's just done some absolutely stellar work for us. And we leaned in and we continued to provide what we think is almost our primary goal as a brand before a product, which is nutritional education and knowledge from the experts that work for us as athletes or on the team itself.\n\nCole Heilborn (23:59): Interesting. You focus more on the content marketing side of things because you didn't have product to sell. Is that what you guys did?\n\nLiz Esche (24:08): Yeah, I would say we leaned into, I mean, we got to sell product at the end of the day, but in order to keep that top of funnel really flush, we want to keep bringing people in. We don't want to stop everything just because we're going through some painful moments. We definitely leaned into the content. We have some really incredible partners in the industry. We're a sponsor of USA climbing. We work with vacation races, we work with Circ Series A vipa, so we continue to support those partners, those efforts, develop those relationships. We leaned heavily on our athlete team. We have an athlete team of over a hundred athletes, and they're just great people and they are the best when talking about our product because they're the ones using it every single day to go out and do incredible things.", "token_estimate": 501, "speakers": ["Liz Esche", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "22:39", "end_timestamp": "24:08", "podcast_index": 90, "episode_name": "Ep 90: Liz Esche | Marketing Through a Re-Brand | Gnarly Nutrition", "slug": "ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Liz Esche", "guest_title": "Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Liz Esche, the Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition joins me to discuss the re-brand project Gnarly rolled out.", "podcast_length": "39:50"}, {"id": "40397443-2d1f-4834-8b2c-d5c4ef611f64", "episode_id": "d91752cb-569f-4180-97ae-74ffb79fc9b8", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4883", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Cole Heilborn (25:00): Okay. You mentioned that because product was limited, it sounds like you pivoted a little bit more towards the educational style of content. Was that to lessen sales but still keep cycle the funnel churning? Or what was the motivation behind that?\n\nLiz Esche (25:18): Yeah, so when you have minimal product to sell, you lean on people like your content manager, Tim Baac, great guy, very skilled. In order to keep that top of funnel just flush, we wanted to keep providing value. Even if we weren't able to give them our top selling product at that exact moment, we still wanted to communicate with them. And we've always believed ourselves to be an education first company. It's in the nutritional arena. A lot of folks just don't even know what they're supposed to be using or how they're supposed to be using it. So those are conversations that we're very used to having and we continued to create those conversations and we did that on our social accounts. We had webinars, we host clinics, we support our partners very in depth. We're a sponsor of USA climbing, we work with vacation races and we make sure that all of the people they're touching have access to our information and our knowledge. (26:24): And we also leaned on our athlete team. We have about a hundred athletes who are just filled with their own additional knowledge and their own expertise using our product daily because they use it pretty much every day to do incredible things. So we reached out to them, we got their buy-in, they were creating more content for us. We really used that time to keep our, we already had a great content machine going, but to make it even better to what can we do even more than we are right now? How can we make this better? How can we make this more accessible? A lot of times we were creating content and we felt like it was getting to our core audience, the people who already know to come to us with their questions. And so we were really focused on pushing that out, putting advertising money behind, pushing that content out versus as much focus just on quick product sales.", "token_estimate": 528, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Liz Esche"], "start_timestamp": "25:00", "end_timestamp": "25:18", "podcast_index": 90, "episode_name": "Ep 90: Liz Esche | Marketing Through a Re-Brand | Gnarly Nutrition", "slug": "ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Liz Esche", "guest_title": "Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Liz Esche, the Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition joins me to discuss the re-brand project Gnarly rolled out.", "podcast_length": "39:50"}, {"id": "3603b327-ed07-409d-9f91-efc48ce42e6f", "episode_id": "d91752cb-569f-4180-97ae-74ffb79fc9b8", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4883", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Cole Heilborn (27:24): Did you learn anything about the brand through the rebrand process that you didn't anticipate learning or discovering?\n\nLiz Esche (27:33): I would say I really learned how much people appreciate companies doing the right thing. We expected. I was mentally prepared for so much backlash with all the change and just leaving people a little bit stranded and not having the exact flavor that they wanted and raising prices and we didn't have that experience. They were willing, and I think that shows kind of the outdoor industry, but also consumers today, they want to work with companies that are doing the right thing. They want to support smaller companies that are taking big leaps in making the effort. And that also came through in our athlete team. We asked them to support us when times were a little bit rough and chaotic, and they were 100% behind us spreading the message of the sustainability and backing us up and saying, this is worth it, this is worth it. And we'd get emails from them that were like, you know what? It's okay. Don't send me product this month, give it to your customer, but we're totally behind what we're doing and we want to support you in whatever way we can. So that I think really surprised me and that lends credit to, I think the early founders of gnarly taking those values seriously and building great relationships with our athletes and our partners.\n\nCole Heilborn (29:08): Right on what, in what way did you grow the most?", "token_estimate": 372, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Liz Esche"], "start_timestamp": "27:24", "end_timestamp": "29:08", "podcast_index": 90, "episode_name": "Ep 90: Liz Esche | Marketing Through a Re-Brand | Gnarly Nutrition", "slug": "ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Liz Esche", "guest_title": "Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Liz Esche, the Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition joins me to discuss the re-brand project Gnarly rolled out.", "podcast_length": "39:50"}, {"id": "078074a8-913f-41ff-ba86-ce4140f40f17", "episode_id": "d91752cb-569f-4180-97ae-74ffb79fc9b8", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4883", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Liz Esche (29:12): Oh, so many ways. This has really been the best experience in understanding more way beyond the marketing aspect because it's not just about marketing, it's about the company as a whole and seeing how marketing fits into that process and how we can do more than just focus on conversion rates and cost of acquisition. How can we focus on the bigger picture, who we are as a company? That has been a huge growth phase for me. It is also taught me how to deal with change and how to respond quickly and stay nimble if we need to. In the beginning, I think I kind of had this idea that I was going to kick all of our marketing spend or a lot of it and save all the money for when all of our ducks were in a row and we could just really come out with a bang and be like, this is gnarly. And that did not happen. And I realized you have to create stepping stones and be able to continue to push your brand and continue to create that story and talk to your customers even when it feels like everything's on fire.\n\nCole Heilborn (30:30): Yeah, no, well said. For someone else or a team, anyone who's trying to do a rebrand or is thinking about doing a rebrand, what would you impart on them? As someone who just went through this?\n\nLiz Esche (30:44): Be extra realistic about your timelines. I think everything takes longer than you think it will, even if you're a smaller company. And I would expect delays and have contingency plans for those delays, and just be transparent with your customers about what's happening and embrace the awkwardness if you have some weird mixed branding. Okay, tell your customers why, show it off. Be like, Hey, this is the last 10 of this product with this label. Kind of like what you said, it's an exclusive item. Now stay lighthearted and keep your head up. It's going to be a little bit goofy and it will be a little bit painful, but you'll get through it.\n\nCole Heilborn (31:38): What do you hope the new brand will communicate to the world?", "token_estimate": 499, "speakers": ["Liz Esche", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "29:12", "end_timestamp": "31:38", "podcast_index": 90, "episode_name": "Ep 90: Liz Esche | Marketing Through a Re-Brand | Gnarly Nutrition", "slug": "ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Liz Esche", "guest_title": "Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Liz Esche, the Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition joins me to discuss the re-brand project Gnarly rolled out.", "podcast_length": "39:50"}, {"id": "3af2c3d6-429f-4e95-a6b7-0c55ed205cc6", "episode_id": "d91752cb-569f-4180-97ae-74ffb79fc9b8", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4883", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Liz Esche (31:43): I think it better communicates how fun we are just as a team. A branding is so much more fun. I think it definitely has a stronger connection with the outdoor industry and really incorporates that spirit, that gnarly spirit. I mean, we just all want to get out there and find our own gnarly, like what's gnarly to you? And I think it better incorporates that and it shows our values more clearly. We care about sustainability. The cans are just step one. We have some exciting things on the radar for 2022, more change, more excitement that we're working through. So I think it will, it'll show all of that off.\n\nCole Heilborn (32:29): Are those things you can share with us, or do you need to keep those under wrap?\n\nLiz Esche (32:33): I will say, well, we'll mostly keep it under wrap for right now, but we are very, very determined to reduce plastic as much as possible.\n\nCole Heilborn (32:43): Awesome. I'm curious, is there anything else that you want to impart on our audience?\n\nLiz Esche (32:49): Oh, that's kind of a big question. That's kind of a,\n\nCole Heilborn (32:57): Is there anything we didn't cover that you want to cover?", "token_estimate": 290, "speakers": ["Liz Esche", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "31:43", "end_timestamp": "32:57", "podcast_index": 90, "episode_name": "Ep 90: Liz Esche | Marketing Through a Re-Brand | Gnarly Nutrition", "slug": "ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Liz Esche", "guest_title": "Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Liz Esche, the Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition joins me to discuss the re-brand project Gnarly rolled out.", "podcast_length": "39:50"}, {"id": "ab956a7d-dcd8-4aeb-8611-d74a546f2402", "episode_id": "d91752cb-569f-4180-97ae-74ffb79fc9b8", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4883", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Liz Esche (33:01): Let me see. Anything we didn't cover? I think I'd just go back to what the thing I would say, this is something we maybe didn't touch on is, and I've kind of spent the last, I don't know how much time talking about pain points and getting through and over these hurdles. And it may sound scary and it may sound like it's not worth it, but it absolutely is. If your reasons are correct, and I've been a part of many teams over the years that have been afraid to make big changes because of the challenges that it would incur. And the longer you wait, the harder it's going to be. I mean, I can't imagine doing this twice the size or doing this when all the current subscribers we have doubled and tripled, and now the earlier you do it is probably better if your reasons are really good and kicking it down the line and just saying like, oh yeah, we'll do that when we get to this point or when we get here, it may not be the right plan because you're just going to incur more of everything, more customer data, more things that need to be changed over. (34:24): So just take a deep breath, make a plan, cross your fingers and get through it.\n\nCole Heilborn (34:31): Well, no, that's really well said. Good things don't come easily. Sometimes you just got to buckle down and jump in feet first.\n\nLiz Esche (34:39): That's absolutely true.\n\nCole Heilborn (34:40): So I know as we're recording this episode, pieces of the brand have been released. When can we expect everything to be updated? When should we all go and stock gnarly's presence on the web?\n\nLiz Esche (34:56): So I would say a lot of it's been released, but if you want to see the new, I would hop on around anytime after January 15th and just check us out.\n\nCole Heilborn (35:09): Perfect. And if folks want to follow along with you personally or with Gnarly, where can people go to connect?\n\nLiz Esche (35:18): My Instagram is boring, so we won't do that one, but gnarly is@gognarly.com and then all of gnarly's handles are gnarly. Nutrition on Instagram, Twitter, TikTok, we are on TikTok, got to get on TikTok. Everybody. Outdoor industry is missing out. Get on TikTok that you can follow us there.", "token_estimate": 546, "speakers": ["Liz Esche", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "33:01", "end_timestamp": "35:18", "podcast_index": 90, "episode_name": "Ep 90: Liz Esche | Marketing Through a Re-Brand | Gnarly Nutrition", "slug": "ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Liz Esche", "guest_title": "Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Liz Esche, the Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition joins me to discuss the re-brand project Gnarly rolled out.", "podcast_length": "39:50"}, {"id": "344d2e7c-0b1a-44a2-b826-a44c51f21387", "episode_id": "d91752cb-569f-4180-97ae-74ffb79fc9b8", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4883", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Cole Heilborn (35:39): I don't know that I've heard of anyone else who's on TikTok like brand wise\n\nLiz Esche (35:44): And they are missing out. Don't ask me to do a podcast about it. New to it. And there's someone else I can point you in the direction of if you want to, but you need to get on TikTok. I mean, it's overtaking Instagram, it's overtaking pretty much everything. I mean, it's not going away. This isn't clubhouse y'all. It's here to stay.\n\nCole Heilborn (36:06): What type of content? What do you guys produce? How do you, because that's always been the question that everyone asks because how does a brand, what's my voice on TikTok?\n\nLiz Esche (36:16): I think it's even easier for outdoor industry brands because lean on your athletes, lean on your community, show their awesome adventures, show them. We have a slew of nutritionists that do a lot of work for us and help our customers, and we've got tiktoks of them with their favorite recipes with gnarly products. We've got tiktoks of them talking about why they use certain products when you use certain products. If people are interested, then they'll watch it. I mean, they got to kind of test your own brand. I don't know if I can give all encompassing advice, but I mean, if Book Nerds can get on TikTok and have a whole book talk, then the outdoor industry can get involved.\n\nCole Heilborn (37:03): Okay. Well, I know what I'm doing. As soon as we wrap up recording today, I'm going to go check out your guys' TikTok because I don't know, maybe I'm just old. I just don't understand it.\n\nLiz Esche (37:14): We are old and it's okay. We're not that old though. But what's interesting is TikTok is actually over 50% of its users are over the age of 30, which is I think, not what people expect. I think they think it's all Gen Z, which we want to talk to Gen Z as well, so we should be on there, but it's older users as well.\n\nCole Heilborn (37:40): Interesting. Yeah, I would not have guessed that. I would've assumed it's everyone who's between the ages of 16 and 18.\n\nLiz Esche (37:48): I thought the same and I was very much corrected by some experts.", "token_estimate": 533, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Liz Esche"], "start_timestamp": "35:39", "end_timestamp": "37:48", "podcast_index": 90, "episode_name": "Ep 90: Liz Esche | Marketing Through a Re-Brand | Gnarly Nutrition", "slug": "ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Liz Esche", "guest_title": "Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Liz Esche, the Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition joins me to discuss the re-brand project Gnarly rolled out.", "podcast_length": "39:50"}, {"id": "db8570bf-36fc-4f8a-abc4-ccff7c3683f2", "episode_id": "d91752cb-569f-4180-97ae-74ffb79fc9b8", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4883", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Cole Heilborn (37:54): Okay. Well, Liz, we might have to now do a follow up episode and talk a little bit more about TikTok.\n\nLiz Esche (37:59): Oh, no, no, not with\n\nCole Heilborn (38:00): Me. Well, thank you for sharing the mic with me today. I appreciate you sharing a little bit about the journey that you guys have been on over at Gnarly. And yeah, I'm stoked to see the new website roll out, and I also give a quick shout out. I think you guys have a short film project coming out soon that's being released that people should look out for.\n\nLiz Esche (38:22): Yes, we do. So we are doing a project with SLCA, salt Lake City Climbers Association, and Nathaniel Coleman, who is the Olympic climber that took second in the Olympics, USA climber awesome guy. We have created a film that's talking about the potential UDOT proposals to widen little Cottonwood Canyon in Salt Lake City. It would just wreck a lot of climbing area and the proposals are not good. So we have an event in Salt Lake City at the front on January 12th, and then are launching the film online on the 13th and Big again. I know I've mentioned a couple times Tim Puniak really arranged that project from beginning to end, produce the whole thing, got the SLCA and USA climbing together and Access fund will be there. So kudos to Tim and I hope y'all watch it.\n\nCole Heilborn (39:23): Awesome. Sounds good, Liz. Well have a great rest of your day and enjoy the new year.\n\nLiz Esche (39:28): Thank you, you too.\n\nCole Heilborn (39:29): Okay.\n\nCole Heilborn (39:30): See you. Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learned something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 440, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Liz Esche"], "start_timestamp": "37:54", "end_timestamp": "39:30", "podcast_index": 90, "episode_name": "Ep 90: Liz Esche | Marketing Through a Re-Brand | Gnarly Nutrition", "slug": "ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-90-liz-esche-marketing-through-a-re-brand-gnarly-nutrition", "guest_name": "Liz Esche", "guest_title": "Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Liz Esche, the Marketing Director at Gnarly Nutrition joins me to discuss the re-brand project Gnarly rolled out.", "podcast_length": "39:50"}, {"id": "72821c97-1792-491a-a85e-f58511b1c8b9", "episode_id": "c924bc90-12a0-4818-810c-7cefee73c32f", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4882", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Mike Artz (00:01): I can't believe I'm still in business and I can't believe I'm a working professional photographer every day. I'm a bit shocked that somehow it all came together, but they really support each other. You want to be someone that your clients want to call back, right? And that's can't be just a good photo or a good video clip. It's got to be that you made their life easier, you actually got them what they asked for on budget on time.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:27): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Helebo and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Port Side Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:59): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Helebo. Today I'm sitting down with Mike Arts. He's the co-founder of the Public Works and Battery 6 21. Mike, how are you\n\nMike Artz (01:09): Doing? Great. Thanks for having me on the show.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:11): Yeah, thanks for being here. I'm stoked. We can make it happen.\n\nMike Artz (01:14): Alright, let's share some wisdom.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:18): Yeah, let's do it. So to kick things off, you're sitting in an Airstream right now. This is your mobile office. What's up with the setup here?\n\nMike Artz (01:27): Airstream has been an amazing client to us for probably about 10 years now, and we have three in our fleet that we use for a bunch of different production uses, but offsite meetings, fun, whatever it takes, kind of just the mobile office on Wheels. I'm currently sitting behind our building, so I am not very far away, but I just love the calm, quiet, comfy of the Airstream. I had the heat running all morning. It's just toasty in here.", "token_estimate": 522, "speakers": ["Mike Artz", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:01", "end_timestamp": "01:27", "podcast_index": 91, "episode_name": "Ep 91: Mike Arzt | Camera Guy or Business Guy? | The Public Works", "slug": "ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Mike Arzt is the co-founder of The Public Works. Since starting the video production company Mike has experienced a challenge that many freelancers turned business owners face. Am I the camera person or business person?", "podcast_length": "44:113"}, {"id": "e6c6130b-f263-4883-9e2b-e47c789eb113", "episode_id": "c924bc90-12a0-4818-810c-7cefee73c32f", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4882", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Cole Heilborn (01:55): That's awesome. I am going to take a page out of your guys' book at some point in the future and write that off as a business expense. I think that makes sense.\n\nMike Artz (02:02): Clients love comfort.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:04): Totally. So before we jump in, we've got a new segment that we've been doing, it's called the Rapid Fire Segment. I'm going to ask a few quick questions just to kind of set the pace for our conversation. First question, where are you calling in from?\n\nMike Artz (02:20): Denver, Colorado.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:21): Okay, and you're in the production business. If you had to choose between Canon or Nikon, which would you choose?\n\nMike Artz (02:28): This isn't going to be a short answer. I've shot Nikon my entire life because my dad shot Nikon and I think I'm still using a couple lenses that he sort of gifted to me. So from that standpoint, I've always been Nikon. Some of our crew is, we're also Cannon and Sony and GoPro and Red. I do love the Nikon. I think their customer service kind of sucks. So I would say I'm a committed consumer. I'm not always a very happy consumer.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:56): Okay, fair\n\nMike Artz (02:56): Enough. Choose wisely. Once you go down a path, it's really hard to change.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:01): I know. Yeah. Once you buy all your lensing, having to swap over it to a different mount is a real pain and using an adapter just isn't convenient. So yeah, it's really a commitment for life. Are you goofy or regular?\n\nMike Artz (03:14): Regular.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:15): Okay.\n\nMike Artz (03:16): But I ski sometimes now too.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:19): Alright, so neither\n\nMike Artz (03:20): Don't tell too many people. Snowboard friends seem sensitive to that\n\nCole Heilborn (03:23): And carbon or aluminum\n\nMike Artz (03:25): On, which\n\nCole Heilborn (03:26): I was imagining a bike, but if you have a different,\n\nMike Artz (03:29): I'm sitting in an aluminum trailer, aluminum is one of the most recycled materials. I think there's a lot of cool stuff about aluminum and I think on bike frames I've often lean that way too. So I'm going to go aluminum.", "token_estimate": 535, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Mike Artz"], "start_timestamp": "01:55", "end_timestamp": "03:29", "podcast_index": 91, "episode_name": "Ep 91: Mike Arzt | Camera Guy or Business Guy? | The Public Works", "slug": "ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Mike Arzt is the co-founder of The Public Works. Since starting the video production company Mike has experienced a challenge that many freelancers turned business owners face. Am I the camera person or business person?", "podcast_length": "44:113"}, {"id": "62b41a88-59a1-4503-bb05-20b21125542e", "episode_id": "c924bc90-12a0-4818-810c-7cefee73c32f", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4882", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Cole Heilborn (03:41): Okay, right on. So I'd love if you could give us a quick background on the public works, what you guys do there, and then kind of a quick breakdown of Battery 6 21 as well.\n\nMike Artz (03:52): Okay. I tried to get better with my quick breakdown because I'm usually a rambler, but my business partner and I, Frank Phillips, we met working at Burton Snowboards over 20 years ago. Frank was the head of engineering and overseeing the prototype shop. I was more on the marketing kind of team side of things. Frank and I became great friends. I moved into his house. He had just bought, we had a big shop there. We were always building stuff, tinkering. I'd be out in the field with the team. I would take that feedback back to him and the development crew. So I don't know. The way I summed it up now is you take the two of us, we've had 20 years of just getting to sit on chairlifts and dream and hike and dream and snowboard and you take the mind of me being a bit more on brand and content and Frank more on design, fabrication, custom build and 20 years go by, start a couple businesses and that's where we are now. (04:52): That's my quickest way of trying to explain how what we do actually makes sense because the Public works is a pretty unique agency. I like to say. I think what sets us apart is we do a lot of the creative work that creative brand agencies would do, but we also do a lot of execution. We have our own fabrication shop, we have photo video team editors, our own studio. So we're a very executional bunch of creatives. I think that sums up the public works heavy outdoor industry, but working with a bunch of different kinds of companies we just love, it's more about the spirit of the company than what they put out the door or whatever. So quite a range of types of clients. Battery 6 21 is a creative workspace. We don't say cowork. We bought this building in Denver about 12 years ago and it was 30,000 square foot empty building. (05:50): It was super intimidating. I mean Frank and I were still working out of our houses, so it was a big kind of step, but our was to find a cool space that we could actually have our workshop and photo crew and studio and have all of that under one roof, but we wanted to be surrounded by other awesome people and companies. We didn't want to just be sitting in a building by ourself. So that's kind of how Battery started. The whole co-work kind of. We were a bit on the, I'd like to think on the front edge. We by no means invented it, but I will say we launched Battery at the same time WeWork launch. So we were at the front edge of the whole thing, battery. We have about 20 tenants now. We're in the busiest intersection in Denver. Stop in anytime and everyone's around, come by and grab a coffee or whatever. But battery, I felt like we dreamed huge and then it exceeded so much of what we ever hoped for with just the events we've been able to host nonprofits, just the random collision of, I mean Denver is such, Denver and Colorado are such great places to begin with and I think what we've got is just one little 30,000 square foot spot that captures a little bit of that and then just good things happen.", "token_estimate": 801, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Mike Artz"], "start_timestamp": "03:41", "end_timestamp": "03:52", "podcast_index": 91, "episode_name": "Ep 91: Mike Arzt | Camera Guy or Business Guy? | The Public Works", "slug": "ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Mike Arzt is the co-founder of The Public Works. Since starting the video production company Mike has experienced a challenge that many freelancers turned business owners face. Am I the camera person or business person?", "podcast_length": "44:113"}, {"id": "5a74ac0a-ba12-401f-8acb-429e0d7d0fbe", "episode_id": "c924bc90-12a0-4818-810c-7cefee73c32f", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4882", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Cole Heilborn (07:04): Right on. So you're an agency, you do creative, you do production work, but you also do fabrication. That's kind of an interesting service to provide. What does that look like in the day to day?\n\nMike Artz (07:15): Well, let's see, I think we're 16 maybe we're 17 years in now with Public Works. So at the beginning it looked like me and Frank just saying yes to everything. You need some photos? All right. Red Bull was one of our first clients, so we did a bunch of really cool custom infrastructure type stuff, like the kind of things you see for Red Bull on Hill branding at one of their ski events or DJ booth that would go to a club that they work with or custom bars, trophies. We did so much different stuff. I think we lucked out. Red Bull hit us up before we'd even launched public works. The first things we did, it was just me and Frank and the shop he built at his house, and I think what Red Bull saw in us was that we could be creative. We really listened to what they had to say about their brand and when they shared the look of the brand, red Bull is probably one of the best companies at controlling the look and feel and execution of their brand. (08:21): So to get to work with them early on, it challenged us, but it also gave us opportunity to make these beautiful pieces. And like I said, we hadn't even launched a company yet, so all of a sudden we just became, these guys call them, they'll build it. I mean we built an arch over a racetrack, you name it, and that was just me and Frank we're like, we got to go weld this. So we'd weld it, we'd build it. I mean that arch we built over the racetrack. Frank and I worked seven days straight and built this 60 foot steel arch powder, coated it, delivered it, installed it and shot photos of it and the race started two hours later and that was the beginning of the public works is it's just sort of that entrepreneurial spirit where even if you're smart enough to say no, you don't, it's all in. So that's kind of how it started. We just got to be known as if you have this sort of crazy idea, then call Mike and Frank. And so that was then. Now we have people that are so much better at almost every aspect of the business than we are. Frank has an amazing design build crew. I've got a media team that I don't even know how to use some of the cameras we have now, which is funny. We're pretty hands on.", "token_estimate": 601, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Mike Artz"], "start_timestamp": "07:04", "end_timestamp": "07:15", "podcast_index": 91, "episode_name": "Ep 91: Mike Arzt | Camera Guy or Business Guy? | The Public Works", "slug": "ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Mike Arzt is the co-founder of The Public Works. Since starting the video production company Mike has experienced a challenge that many freelancers turned business owners face. Am I the camera person or business person?", "podcast_length": "44:113"}, {"id": "7193c9aa-9e7a-4b7c-9b2e-0da51a6a6333", "episode_id": "c924bc90-12a0-4818-810c-7cefee73c32f", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4882", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Cole Heilborn (09:37): Yeah. Oh, that's awesome and I'm excited to have you on the show and kind of dive into this position that I think a lot of creative folks find themselves similar to your story, it sounds like we'll get into this a little bit. A lot of people start out, they're just hungry, they love the camera, they love graphic design. Whatever the creative outlet is, they're just excited to create it and as their business grows, they realize they come to a point where they start managing the creative less and they're less hands-on with the camera. And I know for you this transition between am I a camera guy or am the business guy has been a really interesting transition and I'm hoping we can kind of dive into that and kind of flush out how to navigate that transition if folks are in a similar position. So if you could kind of set us up and so we can understand how did you end up at this position? When did you realize this was a decision that you were making? Because you're more on the business side. Correct. Now", "token_estimate": 259, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "09:37", "end_timestamp": "09:37", "podcast_index": 91, "episode_name": "Ep 91: Mike Arzt | Camera Guy or Business Guy? | The Public Works", "slug": "ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Mike Arzt is the co-founder of The Public Works. Since starting the video production company Mike has experienced a challenge that many freelancers turned business owners face. Am I the camera person or business person?", "podcast_length": "44:113"}, {"id": "12ebe1e2-067d-4e95-9b29-04c9a92404e1", "episode_id": "c924bc90-12a0-4818-810c-7cefee73c32f", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4882", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Mike Artz (10:33): It's still one of our primary photographers. I have my FA drone license, so I'm really loving the drone stuff still and video. So I don't want my progression as a content creator to slow down. I want to keep getting better and it's one of the things that makes me super happy is being out in the field. So it is always a balance because I know that we've got crew, I don't need to be on a shoot anymore and they're going to crush. Maybe our crew will do better if I'm not there. I understand that part too, but I am not stopping because I freaking love that. I was just on a fun trip and our client, it was more of a meeting and our client was like, you don't need to shoot this. We're going to have fun, get through some stuff, whatever, but you can't help it. (11:21): Got to have a camera with you and drone. And so I'm not done with that. I think when I look back, I feel like where I really lucked out was young. Back in high school, I got into photography, built a dark room, would go up, snowboard against Stratton, drive all the way home, get right into the dark room and process that role, a tmax 400 that I shot that day and I just love that. And then I love being able to share those images back out to your friends or whoever. So kind of started with a bit of that, but then as I really wanted to chase my career path through the snowboard industry, I got a job at Burton Snowboards and started going to University of Vermont Business School at the same time. So things really shifted more to a business kind of platform. (12:05): For me, Burton was one of the best educations I could have ever hoped for from team management to product to the culture, the culture of that company alone. So I feel like I got to do all those years at UVM Business School. At the same time I was learning from everything Jake had created. It was sort of towards the tail end of my seven years at Burton. I was traveling the world with the athletes and I started taking my camera a bit more and shooting a bit more and started getting some props on the stuff I was shooting from some of the amazing photographers we were out with. It was also the same time when internet, legit internet, internet sites were starting to launch like Blue Torch and Hard Cloud and the snowboard magazines were starting to get sites and stuff. So I was able to start doing some freelance while I was traveling for Burton and all of a sudden that re-lit, maybe I have something here as a content creator also, which I had never really expected. (13:11): I still remember I ran the Burton Mount Hood house for a couple summers out there and the first shot I had published was this drunk Japanese snowboarder guy standing on a table in his underwear, basically doing a table dance and transworld ran it in the tiny type section and I got a $50 check and I was like, holy shit, I can't believe I sold a photo. I still remember back to that and I was blown away. So the tail end of that time at Burton kind of lit a fire under me to pursue the content side of things and that was some of the motivation to leave Burton because I had an amazing job. It was a company I'd always dreamed for working for and it was really hard to leave, but I left to go work for a media company. So then I went towards being snowboard editor and I was over overseeing TV show, still print stuff, web and either I was shooting it or I was buying it, curating it, working with freelancers. (14:15): So that was pretty cool. It just didn't last that long. And then I started looking for the next move. It was during the.com implosion. I got to experience that firsthand. The company I was working for was funded by a chip company and I mean it ended quick. A buddy of mine got hired the week before we were out in Irvine, California. We all go into work on a Monday and they were just like, yeah, it's over. You all need to pack your stuff and get, oh man, that's how fast it imploded there. But my next move, as I was looking around interviewing for different companies, I ended up going with Airwalk snowboarding, which in turn, they're the ones that moved me to Colorado, which I have to always appreciate and thank them for even though it's a pretty shitty brand and that's a long story in of itself, but that I think was a really big turning point for me. (15:05): Even when I interviewed with Air Walk, it was for the global team manager position, but I went in with my photography with some videos I had made. I really sold that side of what I wanted to do and even part of me taking that offer was I asked for a new Nikon camera set up. I'd been still shooting this old camera body my dad had hooked me up with and Airwalk threw down and they saw it and they bought me some kit so that the Airwalk was really cool because it gave me this ability to be on the business side of things and on the content creation side of things. And I was hiring and working with other photographers too, so I got to sit on both sides of the table. I got to learn from some amazing people. Stan Evans, legendary photographer taught me so much through that time. (16:00): So it really was another, it was a career move, but it was even more so it was kind of an education and absorption time for me. When Airwalk finally imploded, that's when I just felt ready to attack on my own. The first freelance stuff I was chasing was very much going after somewhat smaller companies in the snowboard industry where I could sell and say, I can organize your entire photo shoot. I'll take care of all the planning, working with the agency to figure out what the shots are that you need. I'll book the athlete travel, I'll shoot it, I'll hire someone else if you need. I tried to become this more turnkey consultant and did that for a couple years and really when we launched Public Works, it was just sort of a rolling extension. I had my freelance kind of side of the world going and Frank had his design engineer freelance world going, and we both just rolled that together. It'd been a long journey that we've been talking about of where we wanted to get to, but we're very conservative, so it just took us a long time to just, there were a lot of steps and sorry that was a bit of a long answer, but it was the worlds of business and of content and creativity. I lucked out that there were both very strong parts of my life instead of it just being all on one side.", "token_estimate": 1603, "speakers": ["Mike Artz"], "start_timestamp": "10:33", "end_timestamp": "10:33", "podcast_index": 91, "episode_name": "Ep 91: Mike Arzt | Camera Guy or Business Guy? | The Public Works", "slug": "ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Mike Arzt is the co-founder of The Public Works. Since starting the video production company Mike has experienced a challenge that many freelancers turned business owners face. Am I the camera person or business person?", "podcast_length": "44:113"}, {"id": "28f05f4c-0ecb-45b7-a817-8147b5190822", "episode_id": "c924bc90-12a0-4818-810c-7cefee73c32f", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4882", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Cole Heilborn (17:28): Yeah, it sounds like from the beginning really you've had a healthy balance between the two.\n\nMike Artz (17:34): Yeah. I can't believe I'm still in business. I can't believe I'm a working professional photographer every day. I'm a bit shocked that somehow it all came together, but they really support each other. I've worked with some amazing photographers that are just not easy to work with. You want to be someone that your clients want to call back and that can't be just a good photo or a good video clip. It's got to be that you made their life easier. You actually got them what they asked for on budget, on time. It's cool that you're in the same industry because you get it. You can't hit on 90% of the job well and then blow it on something else and think that it's going to work out well over time.\n\nCole Heilborn (18:21): Well, I don't know about you, but sometimes I like to browse other people's portfolios and I think a really strong indicator that is an example of this is you'll see, I think creatives, we're always nitpicking each other's work and sometimes it can be kind of cynical and probably not very healthy, but you're nitpicking a competitor's project they did with a client and you're like, oh, I could do that so much better. My creative would execute so much stronger than whatever these people did. And then it took me a while to get to the point where I was like, you know what? There's actually a reason that this client worked with this company. And sure, the creative is really important, but they probably care less about the, I might bite myself if I say this, but the client might care less about the creative and they care more about the relationship and the ability to work with said creative. And so what you don't see on the portfolio is the relationship that that company or that individual has with the client\n\nMike Artz (19:22): And everything that went into it. Maybe there was an agency that was dead set that that was the way it was going to get done and it was squeezed into a super short timeline with a stupid budget and you just never know all the different elements that it takes to get something done", "token_estimate": 545, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Mike Artz"], "start_timestamp": "17:28", "end_timestamp": "19:22", "podcast_index": 91, "episode_name": "Ep 91: Mike Arzt | Camera Guy or Business Guy? | The Public Works", "slug": "ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Mike Arzt is the co-founder of The Public Works. Since starting the video production company Mike has experienced a challenge that many freelancers turned business owners face. Am I the camera person or business person?", "podcast_length": "44:113"}, {"id": "43f8c831-f5a5-4aa2-8d9a-8e8b93c68b60", "episode_id": "c924bc90-12a0-4818-810c-7cefee73c32f", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4882", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Cole Heilborn (19:40): For you. It sounds like the creative and the business have been balanced pretty naturally throughout your career. I don't know that that's true for everyone though. It feels like one or the other Jekyll Hyde situation. Why is that?\n\nMike Artz (19:54): I think there's something to be said for kind of right brain, left brain of sometimes the best artists. Sometimes you see amazing photographers, but if they have to shoot to a brief or to a brand, look, they might not always be the best person. I think that's a little bit of it. I think sometimes it's just listening in this world, and I'll fault myself for this too. People aren't always good at listening. You often want to go do what you want to do, but when you're in the commercial world, we are. Someone's paying you to do something that's different than me saying, I just want to go out and just shoot the most beautiful photos I can and try to hang 'em in a coffee shop and sell 'em. Right? So I don't know. I think it's interesting dynamic and for Frank and I, Ian was the third member of the team. (20:54): He pretty much hopped on when we launched public works, so he's been with us a long time too. All three of us are definitely creatives. We use a business operating model that it generally breaks people between visionaries and integrators, right? I'm going to get this right. But more or less, Frank, Ian and I all knew that we were kind of more worked on a visionary kind of mindset, but if you don't have the balance of that in the crew of people who actually can execute and stay organized and make sure that little pieces don't fall off, it's real tough. And we struggled with that for a while trying to find those right people really in our world, they tend to be the producers or the chief operating officer or whatever, but that's harder to find than visionary. It's easy to find the dreamers. I think it's a little harder to find the people who actually see it through and execute. I think that's part of that balance. Having an amazing CO slash producer right now, the amount of freedom that gives me to be able go chase a new client or go be on a shoot and execute. It's a total feeling of freedom.", "token_estimate": 550, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Mike Artz"], "start_timestamp": "19:40", "end_timestamp": "19:54", "podcast_index": 91, "episode_name": "Ep 91: Mike Arzt | Camera Guy or Business Guy? | The Public Works", "slug": "ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Mike Arzt is the co-founder of The Public Works. Since starting the video production company Mike has experienced a challenge that many freelancers turned business owners face. Am I the camera person or business person?", "podcast_length": "44:113"}, {"id": "8eadf320-5535-40c3-83e2-23f4e246da8c", "episode_id": "c924bc90-12a0-4818-810c-7cefee73c32f", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4882", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Cole Heilborn (22:12): So you guys have an operating officer or an executive producer who's managing that stuff for you?\n\nMike Artz (22:19): Yeah, a couple. And we also have the building management, so there's a lot going on, but we have some great people now that help execute and aren't afraid to tell us where we're not holding up to our side of things.\n\nCole Heilborn (22:36): Sure. Yeah, I mean that's a really interesting subject in itself. So I guess I have two questions here. My first question is, so for folks who aren't in a position to go out and hire someone or who don't want to hire someone, how can they personally try to do a better job of staying organized? Was there a point in your guys' career where you couldn't afford to bring someone on so you had to figure out how to get organized yourself?", "token_estimate": 204, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Mike Artz"], "start_timestamp": "22:12", "end_timestamp": "22:36", "podcast_index": 91, "episode_name": "Ep 91: Mike Arzt | Camera Guy or Business Guy? | The Public Works", "slug": "ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Mike Arzt is the co-founder of The Public Works. Since starting the video production company Mike has experienced a challenge that many freelancers turned business owners face. Am I the camera person or business person?", "podcast_length": "44:113"}, {"id": "d2a55f77-12b4-457e-9b65-75c332c4f9b6", "episode_id": "c924bc90-12a0-4818-810c-7cefee73c32f", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4882", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Mike Artz (23:01): For sure. I think getting really good systems in place from early on matters so much, and if that's an area that means spending a little bit of money. We did all our own QuickBooks, like all our own accounting for a long time, and then we'd hand the books off to a fractional CFO to tune them in, do the filings, that kind of thing. But we waited way too long to hire a fractional bookkeeping, kind of fractional CFO kind of role. We waited too long on that and not only was it time that Frank and I were spending working in QuickBooks that we probably should have been doing other stuff, but we also weren't building systems as well as we should have and getting advice, having another set of eyes that's more financially driven, that kind of thing. When we finally hired that team, there was quite a bit of process to just clean up the way we had been doing things. (23:59): That was one area where, and I still look at how much we spend each month on those services and it's like a standout item, but I also think about how much they're doing for us and how else would we do it. So that's the biggest example that comes to mind for me. I think I can remember being that sole entrepreneur and just trying to land the next shoe and all you're thinking about is shooting the best photos and when you send an invoice and a check comes in, it's the most rewarding feeling in the world that someone paid you for your work. So it's hard to spend when you're at that point, but the certain areas like finance and if you're going to be a professional photographer, videographer or whatever, you've got to have your asset digital asset management super dial too. So whether that's asking around and finding a mentor or actually paying someone for some help, getting that kind of stuff like the foundation of what you do proper from the beginning I think is hard to see when you're at those early days of looking at every dollar. (25:01): But here we are 16 years down the road or something and could really feel the change. When we got more dialed in with those kind of systems, we got multiple photographers shooting, putting all our photos into one Lightroom database and now we can look through a million photos or something pretty quickly and drill in, find them, share 'em, use 'em and know that we're triple backed up and that kind of stuff that I think was at least hard for me to see far enough out at the beginning and probably not invest in help. And now we're in this awesome world where whatever service you need, whether you need an hour of help or a week or someone on retainer who lives in Bangladesh and feels like they're sitting next to you, everything's available now. Right. There's almost no excuse anymore to just finding some good help, man. I got to start trying to give you some shorter answers, but", "token_estimate": 709, "speakers": ["Mike Artz"], "start_timestamp": "23:01", "end_timestamp": "23:01", "podcast_index": 91, "episode_name": "Ep 91: Mike Arzt | Camera Guy or Business Guy? | The Public Works", "slug": "ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Mike Arzt is the co-founder of The Public Works. Since starting the video production company Mike has experienced a challenge that many freelancers turned business owners face. Am I the camera person or business person?", "podcast_length": "44:113"}, {"id": "e7b3694c-ae7e-46c2-8e98-ddf7b08795e1", "episode_id": "c924bc90-12a0-4818-810c-7cefee73c32f", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4882", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Cole Heilborn (26:05): No, this is great. So you mentioned that you felt like you guys waited too long before you started hiring help. When is the right time? When did you realize it was too late or not too late but that you should have done it earlier?\n\nMike Artz (26:18): I'm sure. I can't imagine there's any perfect answer. I would look at it as saying what's the value of your own time and what can it be best used for? We still do a lot of our own QuickBooks entry and stuff ourselves where we could just push it off because on a lot of those expenses and stuff that are shoot driven or we want to put the time into making sure that the accuracy is there, that clients get billed back, that we know where we spent that we're tracking it in QuickBooks, that the way it's going to report for us. So I mean that right there is an ongoing debate we always have of how much time is our team putting into it versus farming it out, trying to value your own time. I think one of the most helpful things for me is we've started talking a lot more about what we do is we have a certain amount of capacity as a company with our core team, we have a sense of how busy can we be, how much can we do? (27:19): For me, I have to look at it the same way. How much am I willing to work? How many hours do I have? So with that capacity of time that I have, what is my best use of time? And if I'm going to drop a FedEx off or I don't want to belittle anything, but if I'm doing that instead of calling a potential client lead, it shouldn't be a hard decision. There's always going to be gray area. I mean, I'll probably leave this Airstream and end up cleaning up a pile of dog poop behind the building or something. But you also, you care about where you are and you also don't want to walk by something like that. But while I'm cleaning it up, I'll be like, is this really what I should be doing with my time? (28:04): I think that's the way I keep trying to think about it. And then to finish that thought, someone, another good friend of mine, we've talked about this capacity thing a lot, but it also gives you a sense if we're going to do a retainer with a client, then we look at, okay, how much time is that going to take up of our crew for the year and then we can budget a bit better, whatever. If our goal for the whole year is X and this is going to take up 20% of our working time, then that better be one fifth of X.", "token_estimate": 605, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Mike Artz"], "start_timestamp": "26:05", "end_timestamp": "26:18", "podcast_index": 91, "episode_name": "Ep 91: Mike Arzt | Camera Guy or Business Guy? | The Public Works", "slug": "ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Mike Arzt is the co-founder of The Public Works. Since starting the video production company Mike has experienced a challenge that many freelancers turned business owners face. Am I the camera person or business person?", "podcast_length": "44:113"}, {"id": "7bb64565-6187-43a9-a294-216c255e036f", "episode_id": "c924bc90-12a0-4818-810c-7cefee73c32f", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4882", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Cole Heilborn (28:35): Yeah. What is the opportunity cost of taking on this project?\n\nMike Artz (28:40): Yeah, exactly.\n\nCole Heilborn (28:41): When you talk about systems, about system development and developing processes to better manage creativity and well just everything that we do and what you guys do, I'm curious how we're developing systems for you guys kind of in a,\n\nMike Artz (28:59): You see tears yet\n\nCole Heilborn (29:03): In a position now where we're starting to develop systems and trying to really organize all of the nitty gritty. We kind of have the high level stuff, but it's a painful process. I'm curious to hear your thoughts on it.", "token_estimate": 165, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Mike Artz"], "start_timestamp": "28:35", "end_timestamp": "29:03", "podcast_index": 91, "episode_name": "Ep 91: Mike Arzt | Camera Guy or Business Guy? | The Public Works", "slug": "ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Mike Arzt is the co-founder of The Public Works. Since starting the video production company Mike has experienced a challenge that many freelancers turned business owners face. Am I the camera person or business person?", "podcast_length": "44:113"}, {"id": "d876e6fd-7f16-4cfb-8f29-069cc3bd45ae", "episode_id": "c924bc90-12a0-4818-810c-7cefee73c32f", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4882", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Mike Artz (29:17): Once again, I don't think there's any easy answer and there's probably no perfect answer. Currently what seems to be working for us is we use Wrike as a project management software only with our team. That's been pretty good. So every project gets tracked, make sure we do things like invoice and get paid, which is always nice at the end, but it just works well too. We can put reminders in of when that project's going to go live with the client, so we remember to push it through our social and support them, whatever. So Wrike has been a really good project management software for a bunch of different people from our crew to all be working on. We just went through our Monday meeting and we go through Wrike and that's our update for Monday. How's each project tracking who needs to follow up, who's getting assigned to do what? It works great for that. We're using Slack as more of our at once communication within our team, but we also have a couple of our biggest clients also in Slack now, which has been really, really good. (30:28): It's just, I think everyone sees Slack and if we see something pop up from our team or from one of those clients and we just know that it's got to be taken care of, it's easy to organize, set reminders, whatever. So Slack's become a bit more of our communication tool when we can. And then, like I said, QuickBooks is our financial, the hard part is trying to set our systems between all of those, right? A project will kick off in Slack, then we have to be like, alright, who's setting it up in Wrike? Did you make sure to assign to me that I'm going to invoice at the end? Then it moves into QuickBooks and at some point if it's not paid, then we see the AR report and QuickBooks, right? It's not perfect. We still screw up. I can be honest on here. I mean there is one client we miss sending an invoice to, and by the time we figured that out, that agency that we had done the job for, it pretty much imploded. (31:25): And let's just say you don't want to make that mistake where you do the work and you don't get paid and it's your own fault. You learn from that and we'll probably do it again at some point. But I mean it's all of that which is how do we make sure we're communicating timely and accurately to clients and that we're tracking the project through. We have some other great tools too. Our head of post-production has made some amazing shared document spreadsheets that for those clients, it's kind of one-stop shopping. They click, they can see every project in process. The ones that have already finished are all, it's all indexed by date, everything. They have links to those projects so they can go and download again. We'll have one tab for video, another tab for the stills so they can look and find any folder of shots. (32:23): Yeah. But to me that's still kind of messed up that we have to, if you count email, text, phone, that's still probably eight different communication channels. The occasional person wanting to use WhatsApp and whatever else, Facebook Messenger and you're like, can we please stop? But there's that many different channels and when we switched to Wrike, we had been in Basecamp before that. We spent a bunch of time with our crew. We really wanted everyone to look at all the different options that were out there, Asana, wrike, Basecamp. We tried to have it be a team decision, but we also said when we decide it's all in, you don't get to question this anymore and if you come on as a sub or whatever, we explain to people early on, this is how we're working and we need you to work in here. And sometimes we'll get some resistance and you'll have the occasional person that's still texting and we're like, please move this conversation with the client into Slack or whatever because it's how things are going to get messed up if we're kind of all over the place. Long answer again, but it's a painful one and it keeps me up at night thinking about, I go into the Monday meeting today and I'm like, man, I haven't heard about this project in a little while or this project. And then the team today everyone's just firing and just giving updates and I was like, okay, everything is on point. So the better the software, the more I sleep the (33:53): And the team using it. Right? You have to embrace it. Yeah.", "token_estimate": 1082, "speakers": ["Mike Artz"], "start_timestamp": "29:17", "end_timestamp": "29:17", "podcast_index": 91, "episode_name": "Ep 91: Mike Arzt | Camera Guy or Business Guy? | The Public Works", "slug": "ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Mike Arzt is the co-founder of The Public Works. Since starting the video production company Mike has experienced a challenge that many freelancers turned business owners face. Am I the camera person or business person?", "podcast_length": "44:113"}, {"id": "89c357ed-dff7-489f-af4f-0e2e6f073f23", "episode_id": "c924bc90-12a0-4818-810c-7cefee73c32f", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4882", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Cole Heilborn (33:56): So what has that been like for you? Coming back to this idea of am I the business guy or the camera guy or a hybrid of both? You've been kind of in a position where you guys have had to develop all of this stuff and figure it out and are still figuring it out, but at the same time, I'm sure there's parts of you where you're just like, I just want to be in the field with a camera not worrying about how we're invoicing clients. What has that been like?", "token_estimate": 120, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "33:56", "end_timestamp": "33:56", "podcast_index": 91, "episode_name": "Ep 91: Mike Arzt | Camera Guy or Business Guy? | The Public Works", "slug": "ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Mike Arzt is the co-founder of The Public Works. Since starting the video production company Mike has experienced a challenge that many freelancers turned business owners face. Am I the camera person or business person?", "podcast_length": "44:113"}, {"id": "297054bc-ea0c-4aa7-b074-419531c2dce8", "episode_id": "c924bc90-12a0-4818-810c-7cefee73c32f", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4882", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Mike Artz (34:21): I think the better the systems and the crew, the more we get to be creative. That stuff gets a little bit out of your way. It varies so much too. If we're going on a shoot and there's only two of us on the crew and it's photo and video, I mean we're doing everything right. We've got to get lunch, we got to do everything. Part of what we try to explain to clients is with, if you're willing, invest a little bit more in the shoot and our team can get a little bit broader. It does change a lot when Ian gets to be just director, then he gets to just think through a creative mindset and just focus on what is the best deliverable we can get out of this shoot if he's got to be there just because it's smaller job or the nature of the beast or whatever. (35:10): But if he's got to be there and be director and kind of producer and still guy, your mental focus keeps getting pulled in different directions and it's been a place I've had to really listen to the team because I lean more towards come from the snowboard background. We're going to go out into the backcountry or whatever you want the leanest, tightest crew, no one's doing craft services on a freaking backcountry snowboard shoe. You just hope everyone brought a cliff bar and you get through the day. But when shoots get a little bit bigger and budgets get a bit bigger, that is the beauty of getting to specialize, getting to focus, and it goes right to post of if you're going to invest a bit more, we can put more time into color, more into sound sweetening. It's all the little steps. And if your budget's 10 grand, that's fine. (36:06): We'll figure it out. You just can't get all of that. If the budget's 200 grand, then we can really think about where it gets spent, how it gets spent, and you get some advantages out of that. So I do think it's an interesting place, and I'm sure you've felt that where you're in the middle of a shoot and you're in this creative focus and the light's good and everything's coming together, and so you get a phone call or something else, and I know there's some theory about when you get distracted while you're in a task, how long it takes you to get back into it. I think I forget what the smart people say, like 12 or 15 minutes or something, but that's real when it's happening over and over", "token_estimate": 580, "speakers": ["Mike Artz"], "start_timestamp": "34:21", "end_timestamp": "34:21", "podcast_index": 91, "episode_name": "Ep 91: Mike Arzt | Camera Guy or Business Guy? | The Public Works", "slug": "ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Mike Arzt is the co-founder of The Public Works. Since starting the video production company Mike has experienced a challenge that many freelancers turned business owners face. Am I the camera person or business person?", "podcast_length": "44:113"}, {"id": "43968f36-bee3-406d-b9a9-a9e52def34d0", "episode_id": "c924bc90-12a0-4818-810c-7cefee73c32f", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4882", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Cole Heilborn (36:51): And by that time, golden hour is gone and you've missed your moment\n\nMike Artz (36:55): And no one has dinner.\n\nCole Heilborn (36:57): Right? It's interesting, those types of tasks. We were on a shoot a couple of months ago and we had, fortunately we had someone, an amazing line producer who helped manage all the talent for the day, helped manage craft our schedules, all of that stuff. And I wish it happened more because it was so amazing to be able to have someone who could just focus on those types of details. And it sounds like, oh, figuring out what to do for dinner. That sounds like a really relatively simple thing, but for whatever reason, when you're in production mode, you're making so many decisions all the time that coming up with something like that in a timely manner and scheduling it for 15 people at a local restaurant, that takes time and it takes effort and it takes planning. And sometimes those details do get missed if you are doing everything.\n\nMike Artz (37:58): The difference of having budget and time to do an on location scout at the right times a day before you just show up to shoot what you get out of it is an amazing difference. When you go in with you've already seen, you know what you're going in to do. That's an area I feel like we're constantly trying to push. And also talent. It's like, I get it, you have friends that can stand in, but there's a difference between a professional model or actor, actress, whatever, and someone's friend. And we will say to clients, okay, this is a $40,000 shoot. Do you really want to save a thousand bucks on talent? And then you have the friend who all of a sudden can't get the day off from work or shows up late or there's a lot. It's easy to watch Zoolander and just laugh and modeling can be funny, but when you work with people who are the master of their craft and they see what you're doing as the photographer, they're reacting to it, they're moving, they're doing different things. When you get to work with professionals, you get professional results.", "token_estimate": 518, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Mike Artz"], "start_timestamp": "36:51", "end_timestamp": "37:58", "podcast_index": 91, "episode_name": "Ep 91: Mike Arzt | Camera Guy or Business Guy? | The Public Works", "slug": "ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Mike Arzt is the co-founder of The Public Works. Since starting the video production company Mike has experienced a challenge that many freelancers turned business owners face. Am I the camera person or business person?", "podcast_length": "44:113"}, {"id": "29256771-9896-4c8c-a39b-697f6ec22c7b", "episode_id": "c924bc90-12a0-4818-810c-7cefee73c32f", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4882", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Cole Heilborn (39:19): It really is amazing to be able to watch a talented actor do their thing because it just makes everything come alive and it looks natural too. Well, Mike, we've chatted about a variety of different topics today. I think this has been a really good kind of maybe even just like a case study of these are the things going on in your world as you're balancing the dichotomy between creativity and business. Do you have any other lasting thoughts or any other pieces of wisdom and advice or I don't know, things you wish you would've done differently?", "token_estimate": 143, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "39:19", "end_timestamp": "39:19", "podcast_index": 91, "episode_name": "Ep 91: Mike Arzt | Camera Guy or Business Guy? | The Public Works", "slug": "ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Mike Arzt is the co-founder of The Public Works. Since starting the video production company Mike has experienced a challenge that many freelancers turned business owners face. Am I the camera person or business person?", "podcast_length": "44:113"}, {"id": "159129ce-3716-463a-ac9a-6b2af09b7656", "episode_id": "c924bc90-12a0-4818-810c-7cefee73c32f", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4882", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Mike Artz (39:52): I should have started with a disclaimer thing. Just because I'm on this podcast does not mean that I am an expert in any of these things we talked about. We're all constantly learning, and I mean just getting to know you and for us to share time, we learn from each other. That's never ending. So I don't want anyone to listen to this and think I'm sitting here trying to speak some professor of pulling this off every day that we get to do what we do and someone pays us. I'm super thankful, but I also know we have a long ways to go and learning. So I mean, that's some of my advice is just surrounding yourself with the best possible people resources. I joined a global entrepreneurial organization that there's about 13,000 business owners in the world that are part of this group called eo, and that has been a game changer for me. I've been in about eight years, but the people I've met, what I've learned, it kind of feels like just one more step in the education process. (40:51): A lot of those people have helped me get through some really tough days where you just feel as an entrepreneur, you feel like the world is caving in on you and you need resources. I think everyone needs those kind of resources. So I think that's one thing is just finding those amazing people. They're everywhere and people want to help each other. You just need to lean into it and look for it. That's one. I feel anytime that we've been able to give back or put our skills towards helping other people. Once again, we have a long ways to go with this, but we work with a couple organizations that we've been able to use some of our media skills or whatever to help support them. Anything we've been to do where we're trying to help other people, I feel it has always come back tenfold in morale for the crew or new business leads or people out there in need that just see that we're a group of caring people and we're trying to help. (41:49): So the give back part I think is huge. I want to do so much more of that. I think it's a unique place that it's one thing to give 1% of revenue or whatever, but when you look at what you can actually do as a skill and how we can help, it's often, I think we've been able do a lot more by putting ourselves towards these tasks. I think those are two of my favorite things. The family personal work balance is this never ending? I'm trying to think. I believe my kids, I think my daughter, yeah, my daughter was born right around the time we launched public works. So you're going through this entrepreneurial journey at the same time, you're trying to be a parent at the same time. You're trying to maintain some sanity and do your own thing. And I don't think there is any answer to the balance between work, family, and personal. (42:43): That's a place that the entrepreneurial group has really been helpful for me, but I feel anytime you're winning on one side of that triangle, the other two sides are suffering. But anytime all three parts get to come together, if I can take my kids on a shoot or to me that's the best day ever. If it feels good for me and it kind of connects to my family and work, that's the ultimate. So the reason I say that is because back when I was starting this thing, I wasn't thinking that way. Now I'm sitting here and my daughter's going to be out of high school in a year. Have I embraced every moment I could have, but I wasn't thinking that way 16 years ago as I was trying to define the life I wanted to lead. And so, I don't know, maybe that's three areas.", "token_estimate": 888, "speakers": ["Mike Artz"], "start_timestamp": "39:52", "end_timestamp": "39:52", "podcast_index": 91, "episode_name": "Ep 91: Mike Arzt | Camera Guy or Business Guy? | The Public Works", "slug": "ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Mike Arzt is the co-founder of The Public Works. Since starting the video production company Mike has experienced a challenge that many freelancers turned business owners face. Am I the camera person or business person?", "podcast_length": "44:113"}, {"id": "876e864e-63be-4897-84a5-a6cc27dd48cd", "episode_id": "c924bc90-12a0-4818-810c-7cefee73c32f", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4882", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Cole Heilborn (43:31): Yeah, no, that's great, man. Yeah, I mean it's never ending. It just keeps going. You keep learning, you keep trying new things, and I appreciate you sharing some of that stuff.\n\nMike Artz (43:39): Yeah, it's been really fun. Always open to talk more with folks too. And\n\nCole Heilborn (43:44): Yeah, Mike, so if folks have, I dunno, you mentioned a couple resources so far in the show today. Any other resources that you'd recommend people look into if they're trying to navigate some of these conversations?", "token_estimate": 133, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Mike Artz"], "start_timestamp": "43:31", "end_timestamp": "43:44", "podcast_index": 91, "episode_name": "Ep 91: Mike Arzt | Camera Guy or Business Guy? | The Public Works", "slug": "ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Mike Arzt is the co-founder of The Public Works. Since starting the video production company Mike has experienced a challenge that many freelancers turned business owners face. Am I the camera person or business person?", "podcast_length": "44:113"}, {"id": "dadb6081-f618-40ca-b857-4ff0ffe3691b", "episode_id": "c924bc90-12a0-4818-810c-7cefee73c32f", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4882", "chunk_index": 19, "content": "Mike Artz (43:59): A SMP has been okay for me, the American Society of Media photographers. I think I could have gotten even more out of it if I was just more active with it. But if you're a photographer or a filmer or an assistant, being an assistant is a great way to get out and learn from other people. A SMP has some good resources. They also have a lot of documents. When you become a member, it's not that expensive, maybe 400 bucks a year or something. They have a lot of documents that help you with your talent releases and they have people in Washington lobbying to protect our rights, copyright, that kind of thing. So A SMP has been pretty good. When I was first freelancing, I became a member of my local Chamber of Commerce and it was cool. Once again, it was a couple hundred bucks a year. (44:47): I met a lot of people in my community of Evergreen, Colorado that it grounded me a bit. And when you join some of those groups that people really start looking out for each other. You want to support the people that you met there and stuff. So me and a couple buddies started talking about Join the Elks Lodge. Now I feel like that's such a, I dunno, I dunno if we have to wear crazy hats or what, I don't even know what they do, but we're like, it seems like a cool thing. We should probably join it. (45:15): I think any of those organizations that help to curate random collision of other people that are thinking somewhat like you are worth their weight in gold because anyone who's joined already joined for a reason, you kind of already passed that part in eo. I can bump into any one of those other 13,000 members around the world and we instantly can get into a conversation deeper than most other people I know because you just already know. This organization is about deep sharing and confidentiality and all that already puts aside, it's an entrepreneur and immediately I can be talking to a guy who owns a business in Catman de and it's on immediately. So I think just looking and looking and finding those other people in your community who you aspire to be or you like what they're doing and yeah, see what they're connected to. And it could be like a Wednesday meetup and go snowboarding group. Everything gets solved and planned and figured out on a chairlift.", "token_estimate": 573, "speakers": ["Mike Artz"], "start_timestamp": "43:59", "end_timestamp": "43:59", "podcast_index": 91, "episode_name": "Ep 91: Mike Arzt | Camera Guy or Business Guy? | The Public Works", "slug": "ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Mike Arzt is the co-founder of The Public Works. Since starting the video production company Mike has experienced a challenge that many freelancers turned business owners face. Am I the camera person or business person?", "podcast_length": "44:113"}, {"id": "e5dcc96b-4f01-491e-9d41-685a016bda3a", "episode_id": "c924bc90-12a0-4818-810c-7cefee73c32f", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4882", "chunk_index": 20, "content": "Cole Heilborn (46:28): I like that. That's a great business model. Mike, if folks want to follow along with you personally or with any of your companies, where can they go to connect?\n\nMike Artz (46:37): My last name is spelled A RZT. Not to be confused with any other way, but my Instagram's Arts m, the Public Works, has got its own Instagram battery, 6 21 in Denver. Keep an eye, like I said, come through, we have tons of different events or just stop through and check it out. LinkedIn, I still think is to me one of the best platforms to actually have professional interaction. I dunno what are all the other channels. I'm still on Facebook. I know I probably shouldn't be getting on the bandwagon of hating on Facebook. But yeah, anyway, like that track me down. Love meeting new people.\n\nCole Heilborn (47:17): Sounds good, man. Well thanks for sharing your time with me today. And are you working out of the Airstream for the rest of the day or are you jumping back into the studio?\n\nMike Artz (47:25): No, we've got a client meeting. I'm going to be back up in Evergreen for another meeting. I think my dog has passed out next to me, so I'm a little bit jealous if I have a few minutes before my next meeting, I'm probably going to shut my eyes and get horizontal for a minute.\n\nCole Heilborn (47:38): I like it.\n\nMike Artz (47:39): Focus,\n\nCole Heilborn (47:39): I like it.\n\nMike Artz (47:40): But no, I highly recommend to anyone in their journey that in the RV is, it is freedom professionally and personally. It's a good thing.\n\nCole Heilborn (47:50): I like it. Alright man, well have a great rest of your day and take care.\n\nMike Artz (47:54): Yeah, thanks so much for having me on the show and I appreciate all the other episodes you've done. There have been some awesome shares on there.\n\nCole Heilborn (48:01): Appreciate it. Thank you.\n\nMike Artz (48:02): Yeah, later.\n\nCole Heilborn (48:03): Alright, see you.\n\nCole Heilborn (48:06): Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learned something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 542, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Mike Artz"], "start_timestamp": "46:28", "end_timestamp": "48:06", "podcast_index": 91, "episode_name": "Ep 91: Mike Arzt | Camera Guy or Business Guy? | The Public Works", "slug": "ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-91-mike-arzt-camera-guy-or-business-guy-the-public-works", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Mike Arzt is the co-founder of The Public Works. Since starting the video production company Mike has experienced a challenge that many freelancers turned business owners face. Am I the camera person or business person?", "podcast_length": "44:113"}, {"id": "58a3452c-8743-438c-8185-d6f3fe133b09", "episode_id": "704a67f4-7ebe-4752-b628-13b12c4efe4d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4885", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Mason Gravely (00:03): The meeting went horrible compared to what I thought it was going to open up this door, and it was the complete opposite of what I expected and was a total shut door shut on this entire branch of my life, and that's the same day I just happened to run into this woman that was become my wife. I think back, I'm like, wow, that's so crazy that this door totally shutting this other door open, which led to so many other things.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:27): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Helebo and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Portside Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Hilborn. Today I'm sitting down with Mason Graveley. He's the lead adventurer over at Athletic Brewing. He's also the host of the Adventure Sports podcast and he's a Father Mason. How are you?\n\nMason Gravely (01:12): Hey, doing well, Cole. How are you doing?\n\nCole Heilborn (01:15): I'm doing awesome. Thanks for joining me today.\n\nMason Gravely (01:17): Absolutely. No, thanks for having me. I'm excited to talk. It's been a bit since I've been on a show, so it's going to be fun.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:25): What was the last podcast you were on?\n\nMason Gravely (01:27): Oh, I think it was literally like Wake Dad, wake Dad Drink repeat or something. It was with Anthony Palmer. It was a fun show, but it's kind of about being a dad and living life to the fullest, I guess you could say. So yeah, it's been a few months though.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:46): That sounds like a really good episode. It also sounds like a great topic in general.", "token_estimate": 510, "speakers": ["Mason Gravely", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:03", "end_timestamp": "01:46", "podcast_index": 93, "episode_name": "Ep 93: Finding the Balance Between Vision and Expectations | Mason Gravley | Athletic Brewing", "slug": "ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "How do you find a balance between vision and expectations ?So often in today's culture, we're told to set a goal and work tirelessly to it - sometimes to a fault.", "podcast_length": "44:115"}, {"id": "38a1544e-7d6f-436f-9b04-aa65bf212e25", "episode_id": "704a67f4-7ebe-4752-b628-13b12c4efe4d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4885", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Mason Gravely (01:51): Yeah, yeah, for dads and drinking, there was a lot of drinking, so it's cool I get to bring a non-alcoholic beer on, but it's good. I mean, there's plenty of how to guides on this life, but it always helps just learning from other people through audio. I love audio.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:12): Yeah, it's a great medium and I'm excited that we can share this audio medium with people today. We've got a fun conversation, Mason. I appreciated the time we've spent prepping for this call. Before we jump into our topic for today, as folks might've realized it's a new year, so we've been trying a new thing here at the beginning of the show. We have a quick fire segment, which are a handful of quick questions that will hopefully give folks a little bit of an idea of who you are beyond what we're going to discuss today. So first question is, and I guess the kind of unofficial rule is you need to respond with quick answers, so as short as possible.\n\nMason Gravely (02:50): Okay. A letter or two. Sweet.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:53): Yeah, yeah. Or just a sound. A noise would work.\n\nMason Gravely (02:56): A grunt. Okay.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:58): So first question is where are you located?\n\nMason Gravely (03:00): Florida.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:01): Okay, second question. What is your favorite medium other than the podcast? Oh\n\nMason Gravely (03:07): Gosh, audio is great video.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:10): Okay. And third, you work for a non-alcoholic brewing company. What is a fun fact about non-alcoholic beer that people don't know?\n\nMason Gravely (03:20): It's indistinguishable from beer,\n\nCole Heilborn (03:23): And I actually recently discovered that before I even knew you, I tasted some of your guys' beer and I didn't realize it was non-alcoholic until a friend pointed it out. So I can vouch for that entirely. Last question, what is your most memorable podcast episode that you've recorded?\n\nMason Gravely (03:42): Camels.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:43): Okay. You're going to have to elaborate on that a little bit.", "token_estimate": 508, "speakers": ["Mason Gravely", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "01:51", "end_timestamp": "03:43", "podcast_index": 93, "episode_name": "Ep 93: Finding the Balance Between Vision and Expectations | Mason Gravley | Athletic Brewing", "slug": "ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "How do you find a balance between vision and expectations ?So often in today's culture, we're told to set a goal and work tirelessly to it - sometimes to a fault.", "podcast_length": "44:115"}, {"id": "02164a41-8cb1-4347-ba6f-14c7d268345d", "episode_id": "704a67f4-7ebe-4752-b628-13b12c4efe4d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4885", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Mason Gravely (03:45): I actually was just emailing before you someone I'm going to have on again, that trekked across Australia, coast to coast with five wild camels on foot. It took like six months.\n\nCole Heilborn (04:01): Were these camels packing gear or why five of them?\n\nMason Gravely (04:06): Good question. Good question. Why five? I had the same question. This Sophie Matton is her name. She was a news anchor in Australia and did a story segment on feral camels. I didn't know this. I know this is, I hope we're done with the rapid fire because I'm butchering it right now. She did a story as an anchor about feral camels in Australia, and I didn't know this, but there's a million wild camels in the Australian Outback. It is comparable to the wild kangaroo population, which I had no idea, and she just fell in love with camels, left her job, got into camel rescuing and just worked at the Camel farm and wanted to raise awareness for this and wanted to do some big adventures. So she decided to literally just like you, would a wild horse, go and wrangle a wild horse, a Mustang. She did that with camels. Looks a little different, but it's kind of similar and decided to teach them to carry gear and she's walking across Australia and as you know, Australia is a 10th. It's the same size as the us the contiguous us, but it's a 10th of the population and a lot more honestly, emptiness and desert. Her stories are unreal. So I'm excited to have her back on. I heard her story halfway through, so we're going to hear the second half on the show soon.\n\nCole Heilborn (05:29): Wow, that does sound really great. When is that coming out? When can people check that out?\n\nMason Gravely (05:33): Probably a month and a half, two months out. Honestly, probably like your show, a couple months out between, but still got to record that one, but next week show we'll record.\n\nCole Heilborn (05:45): Awesome. I would love if you could give us a quick introduction about yourself, how long you've been the host of the podcast, how long you've been working with Athletic Brewing.", "token_estimate": 521, "speakers": ["Mason Gravely", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "03:45", "end_timestamp": "05:45", "podcast_index": 93, "episode_name": "Ep 93: Finding the Balance Between Vision and Expectations | Mason Gravley | Athletic Brewing", "slug": "ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "How do you find a balance between vision and expectations ?So often in today's culture, we're told to set a goal and work tirelessly to it - sometimes to a fault.", "podcast_length": "44:115"}, {"id": "1dc130e9-3355-4a81-976e-8943ce2be129", "episode_id": "704a67f4-7ebe-4752-b628-13b12c4efe4d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4885", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Mason Gravely (05:54): Oh, absolutely. So I've been hosting Adventure Sports podcast for about three years and the show is coming up on six years old, so we've been going. So I've been the host for about half the life of the show. Funny enough, it's a podcast I bought from somebody. I was a guest on the show and then I could just tell in the host's voice after I was on, and I don't know if I've ever told Kurt the former host this, I just felt like he was a little burned out and so I just shot him an email one day and was like, Hey Kurt, I'm just noticing in your voice, man. I don't know. If you ever need help, let me know. I love podcasts, but it's kind of like anything else. You fall in love with a medium or doing something you don't know if you'd actually enjoy doing it as much as you enjoy consuming it. (06:43): So I just thought I'd give it a shot and he is like, I didn't hear anything from him for a month. I'm like, oh boy, I must've pissed him off or something. And a month later he's like, Hey man, you struck the nail on the head. Both me and the former host, Travis is his name. We both kind of are ready to move into different parts of our lives in a big way because of the influence of the show. We're ready to take on more adventurous lifestyles, but to do that, it takes backing away from the show. And I was entering more of a, I don't know, I was actually moving out of a really mobile, I don't know, vagabond lifestyle and into something more. I just got married and so I was looking into something a little more grounded. So they trained me and I took over the show and through the show one of the listeners was the founder of Athletic Brewing and he found out on the show that I was going to be leaving my other job and backpacking and we just started working together. And here I am two years later with Athletic Brewing, which has been getting an invitation to sit on a rocket ship. You got a seat on one of the rocket ships or one of the seats on the rocket ships to strap in and it has been a wild, wild two years.\n\nCole Heilborn (08:05): So for all podcasters out there, it is possible to sell your podcasts if you come to a point where you're burned out.", "token_estimate": 548, "speakers": ["Mason Gravely", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "05:54", "end_timestamp": "08:05", "podcast_index": 93, "episode_name": "Ep 93: Finding the Balance Between Vision and Expectations | Mason Gravley | Athletic Brewing", "slug": "ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "How do you find a balance between vision and expectations ?So often in today's culture, we're told to set a goal and work tirelessly to it - sometimes to a fault.", "podcast_length": "44:115"}, {"id": "df3a9283-1244-4bde-a2c9-f6303f5b629e", "episode_id": "704a67f4-7ebe-4752-b628-13b12c4efe4d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4885", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Mason Gravely (08:12): Yeah, it totally is. Now they sold it to me at Rock bottom prices. They probably didn't realize it. No, they totally did. They wanted to see the show continue and thankfully the transition went really well and the show has grown, and I do it on the side. It's just literally a side hustle. It's honestly more of a hobby than anything. I'm not trying to make a complete living at it. I just enjoy having conversations with people, especially in a world like this. It was a lifesaver through COVID having this sort of interaction. And there's just not a lot of mediums in today's world where you have an hour long conversation with somebody about their life and their story and what drives them. You definitely feel connected afterwards. You're like, I feel like I know this person. And there's not a lot of places in modern life that you have a quality conversation like that, you know what I mean? With a stranger.\n\nCole Heilborn (09:07): Absolutely. I mean, the podcast format in itself I think just naturally lends itself to when both parties agree to be on the show together. There's kind of this unspoken rule that you're going to be more intimate and you're going to have a real conversation. And I'm not exactly sure why that is. Maybe just this is the trend that's been set and so now people who accept podcast requests, they're those types of people. They enjoy those types of connections.\n\nMason Gravely (09:37): That might be, that's an interesting thought. I've never thought about that, but it is. When I see someone's name as a guest, I expect for them to open up. I literally was listening to a podcast yesterday about it to be on this show. You agree to be an open book. It was Guy Roz with a, gosh, it's a big show. I listen to it all the time, how I built this about founders. You got to say things on here you haven't said before. And I'm like, wow. So you're mentioning the same thing the next day. It's pretty cool.", "token_estimate": 490, "speakers": ["Mason Gravely", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "08:12", "end_timestamp": "09:37", "podcast_index": 93, "episode_name": "Ep 93: Finding the Balance Between Vision and Expectations | Mason Gravley | Athletic Brewing", "slug": "ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "How do you find a balance between vision and expectations ?So often in today's culture, we're told to set a goal and work tirelessly to it - sometimes to a fault.", "podcast_length": "44:115"}, {"id": "1a0f2db0-6e7d-4769-90f2-3df7dee212f3", "episode_id": "704a67f4-7ebe-4752-b628-13b12c4efe4d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4885", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Cole Heilborn (10:12): Well, I'm excited to get into our topic for today. We're going to talk a little bit about the balance between vision and expectations. Mason, you've got kind of a fun, well, I'm sure you did not anticipate ending up where you are today, however, many years ago, and we've been talking a little bit about on the show lately, finding the right career, finding the right spot in life. We've talked to a few different folks in-house who have tried to find the right spot for them in their career. We've talked to creatives about positioning and niching and finding your focus. And so actually this podcast with Mason is actually kind of unintentionally continuing the conversation, but from a little bit of a different perspective, I think so many of us go out and we set dreams and then we work tirelessly to those dreams, and that's not always bad. But Mason, you have kind of a different philosophy as to how to approach some of these ideas. So I guess why is this something that you care about?", "token_estimate": 255, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "10:12", "end_timestamp": "10:12", "podcast_index": 93, "episode_name": "Ep 93: Finding the Balance Between Vision and Expectations | Mason Gravley | Athletic Brewing", "slug": "ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "How do you find a balance between vision and expectations ?So often in today's culture, we're told to set a goal and work tirelessly to it - sometimes to a fault.", "podcast_length": "44:115"}, {"id": "0f71a09a-133a-4575-b69c-2157b87fdd18", "episode_id": "704a67f4-7ebe-4752-b628-13b12c4efe4d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4885", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Mason Gravely (11:14): I hope I don't get in trouble with my day job. Very driven, very focused people, but I feel like an anomaly some days with athletic crew and I work in marketing with them. And I guess the part of marketing I enjoy is the storytelling aspect. But yeah, I would say from an early age I would have passions for a little while and then life would just diverge. For some reason that was out of my control and it just taught me to accept change or accept that you might not know what's best for you. Never really had a clear vision of what I wanted to do. I just really like to explore. To this day, that's my favorite thing to do is it's not a sport per se or it's not a thing. It's more of can I explore through this medium? That's why I like podcasting. (12:07): I can explore new ideas and new people and perspective. That's why I like to bike because you can explore really efficiently, actually explore, and I don't like doing the same thing over and over and over again in the same way necessarily. So I don't know, some early examples, just last week I was playing pool, like billiards with somebody. I was playing like nine ball with a friend at an event and it hit me. I was like, I used to be on a semi-pro circuit for pool as a teenager and I was rising through the ranks of a professional pool player, which is super random and I just fell in love with it. My dad used to take us to bars and stuff when I was a kid and he would go sing karaoke. This is so terrible. I literally have never shared the story before on a podcast especially, and we'd go play pool. So he just loved karaoke. He was a wild man, and we'd go play pool and I got really, really good, me and my stepbrother, and then out of nowhere I just discovered basketball and then that pool was just gone. I was quite literally on track to be a professional winning tournaments, winning money at a young age doing this most nights of the week, staying up very late, practicing fanatically to letting it go to pursue basketball, which brought me into college. (13:32): And then, gosh, another great example, later on in life, I had a very promising meeting one day for a very real career path and partially an adventure. And the meeting went horrible compared to what I thought it was going to open up this door. And it was the complete opposite of what I expected and was a total shut door shut on this entire branch of my life. And that's the same day I just happened to run into this woman that was become my wife. So I guess I've just learned that I think back, I'm like, wow, that's so crazy that this door totally shutting this other door open, which led to so many other things. I guess I just learned that don't hold on to what you think you want too long. You can live by principles and live by and maybe have some sort of end goal in mind. And I think with talking with plenty of adventures and doing plenty of my own adventures, I never expected any of my adventures to actually take place. They all seemed like the ideas for them to come out of left field. So I could have never imagined I'd be working for a non-alcoholic brewery now, and I have no idea what the next 10 years brings. I just know that the principles I want to live by, if those are present, I'll be happy and I'll be content, if that makes sense.", "token_estimate": 830, "speakers": ["Mason Gravely"], "start_timestamp": "11:14", "end_timestamp": "11:14", "podcast_index": 93, "episode_name": "Ep 93: Finding the Balance Between Vision and Expectations | Mason Gravley | Athletic Brewing", "slug": "ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "How do you find a balance between vision and expectations ?So often in today's culture, we're told to set a goal and work tirelessly to it - sometimes to a fault.", "podcast_length": "44:115"}, {"id": "d836fef8-a651-47e1-a6f4-3fe250eed93a", "episode_id": "704a67f4-7ebe-4752-b628-13b12c4efe4d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4885", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Cole Heilborn (15:00): Yeah, that does make sense. So you have an idea of the direction based on your morals of where you want to go or where you're okay going. But in terms of the actual destination, you're okay being surprised by that?\n\nMason Gravely (15:15): Oh, absolutely. Yeah. I hope to be surprised by it. I think of the podcast, I think of my life, I think of our marketing here at Athletic Brewing. I have a degree in biology, so I obviously didn't plan to be in marketing, so I am not much of a planner. I'm not sure you can tell. So the kind of thought that I think about it all is being a river, it's analogy I'm constantly bringing up, I'm telling the people is like a lot of people have their mind set on some sort of pinnacle, some sort of summit, and they fight for that. And there's a mountain on my screen right now you can't see of a mountain. I've climbed one of the fourteeners in Colorado. I love climbing mountains, but that's not how I live my life. I actually prefer being in the alpine. I prefer being down where there's lots of life and lots happening and where those kinds of processes are taking place. And I think that comes into play with my personal life is I just like going where things are flowing the most, where it's making the most sense, where it's not so much struggle all the time, it's going with the flow, it's fighting hard on certain things, but if there's a clear boundary there just going where things are succeeding and things are making sense, we can dive into that. But that's kind of the principle and that's taken me on some really cool twists and turns. I'll tell you that.\n\nCole Heilborn (16:51): Well, it got you to a spot where you bought a podcast and are the lead adventurer, which I'm still not really sure what that means at a non-alcoholic brewing company. I don't know", "token_estimate": 458, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Mason Gravely"], "start_timestamp": "15:00", "end_timestamp": "16:51", "podcast_index": 93, "episode_name": "Ep 93: Finding the Balance Between Vision and Expectations | Mason Gravley | Athletic Brewing", "slug": "ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "How do you find a balance between vision and expectations ?So often in today's culture, we're told to set a goal and work tirelessly to it - sometimes to a fault.", "podcast_length": "44:115"}, {"id": "e671f976-ab4d-483f-b68f-061e0edb6a4c", "episode_id": "704a67f4-7ebe-4752-b628-13b12c4efe4d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4885", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Mason Gravely (17:01): Either. I really don't know. People are like, I see that title and I'm like, first of all, I have no qualifications on being a lead adventure other than living like that. And I think that's honestly what it is. It's being willing to just say, we'll see what happens. And so I do want to tell people there's nothing wrong with having goals. I would say I have a goal, I always have a goal. I just know that that goal is a moving target. It might shift. For instance, I've done a lot of cross country bike rides and there's always the goal to get to the finish line and to get to the end, but all that space in between is fair game. If there's something really interesting to go 10 miles off the path to see a national park, I wasn't anticipating or to see something or to meet somebody or to stay off the path. In fact, when I do these cross country bike rides, I don't bring a cycle computer, I don't keep track of any stats. I just have a goal at the end of each day, I need to get to this town. And sometimes that's not possible based on weather or whatnot, and you still have goals and you still work your butt off. But being open, a great example is this guy I had on the show recently, Seth Alvo. He's known as Seth's Bike Hacks on YouTube. He's a YouTuber.\n\nCole Heilborn (18:27): Yeah.", "token_estimate": 329, "speakers": ["Mason Gravely", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "17:01", "end_timestamp": "18:27", "podcast_index": 93, "episode_name": "Ep 93: Finding the Balance Between Vision and Expectations | Mason Gravley | Athletic Brewing", "slug": "ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "How do you find a balance between vision and expectations ?So often in today's culture, we're told to set a goal and work tirelessly to it - sometimes to a fault.", "podcast_length": "44:115"}, {"id": "dbfae851-cf80-4442-83ca-fbb41bc139a2", "episode_id": "704a67f4-7ebe-4752-b628-13b12c4efe4d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4885", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Mason Gravely (18:27): Okay. You're familiar with him? Yeah, he's got the coolest story. I've watched him through his show and if people present themselves, they do what works for them through the medium they choose. But when you talk to him in a different format, it's crazy how different they are. He was so different than I expected him to be from watching his videos, but he fell in love with mountain biking in Florida of all places. And we just happen to both be in Florida, but in Miami where it's not really well known to be a mountain bike park, even though there are some great bike parks there, there's a lot more elevation change in Florida than people realize. It's pretty cool. But anyway, he fell in love with it and just knew that he wanted to be in cycling, but he didn't know how or what, and he started posting these how to videos and just random stuff on YouTube and it started taking off. (19:15): So he ran with that. To make a long story short, he's built such a big community that he's moved to one of the biggest bike meccas in the country, Brevard North Carolina or just Asheville area, and is building a bike park that his community is paying for. He has a Patreon page, all the money there, it goes to purchasing land to build bike trails and whatnot. And it's like this enormous project. He's working with the city and the state and building a mountain bike hotel. And his whole thing was I wanted to work in this industry somehow. I didn't know the details, but as I pursued that, things began to grow. And so with that big goal in mind, he had no idea what that would look like or where that would go, and he didn't know the details of building a park and where it would be. (20:11): He just knew he loved this thing and wanted to find something within here to pursue. And so I would say for me, it's the similar thing. I don't even have it that focused where it's like a specific topic. I just want to get more people to do adventures is honestly what I do. And so everything I work on moves people toward that, including myself, moves myself towards achieving that. So I really love Seth's story because it's so community focused, it's so opportunistic. And that was the big quote he shared from that episode was don't chase your vision, chase the opportunity on the way to your vision and the opportunity is just it's going to come in ways you don't expect. So just be alert. As my wife jokes with me, I'm always be alert. You never know where that next brick in the road is going to be. (21:11): It's through the most unseeing of circumstances. It's a great example with athletic brewing. One of the biggest opportunities of my life is to work here. And I missed, I think I missed Bill's call twice. I was at outdoor retailer actually in Denver trying to get a job in the outdoor industry, and he was calling me and I'm like, what's this number I'm not going to answer? And thank God he left a voicemail because if he didn't, I would've never called him back. And you know what I mean, you don't expect it. And so I don't know, I think that's really fun to kind of turn over those rocks and think of life in that way that this next encounter, this next stop at a gas station, this next email or whatever, the most mundane task you can think of this next thing could be life changing, how oftentimes life happens.", "token_estimate": 837, "speakers": ["Mason Gravely"], "start_timestamp": "18:27", "end_timestamp": "18:27", "podcast_index": 93, "episode_name": "Ep 93: Finding the Balance Between Vision and Expectations | Mason Gravley | Athletic Brewing", "slug": "ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "How do you find a balance between vision and expectations ?So often in today's culture, we're told to set a goal and work tirelessly to it - sometimes to a fault.", "podcast_length": "44:115"}, {"id": "8f691f5c-4c40-4979-99f2-7df36558bd14", "episode_id": "704a67f4-7ebe-4752-b628-13b12c4efe4d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4885", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Cole Heilborn (22:02): I think that idea too is kind of empowering A few years ago. It's like being self-employed. You have your own set of hurdles and challenges every day, and it's always been over the few years of doing the production business, it's always been something that's just in the back of your mind of like, well, how am I going to pay folks this month? How are we going to find our next job? And you slowly start to answer those questions and figure out ways to solve those problems. But pretty early on I discovered this idea and that you just nailed it. The idea that tomorrow I could get a phone call or an email that could completely change this current problem or this perceived problem that I'm dealing with. And knowing that that possibility was out there was it gave me a sense of I didn't have to worry as much because I knew I still had to put in the work, but I also knew that things can just happen that I didn't plan for. And those moments don't happen all the time, but when they do, they're pretty incredible.\n\nMason Gravely (23:08): It's definitely motivating, especially when times are tough because you'd start digging in anybody's story. Those things seem to be the catalyst for almost anyone remarkable or anyone that's doing something different, even if it's not on a huge scale, it's just like someone you find personally inspiring that you might personally know. A lot of times it is some big break like that that happens along the way because they've been consistent in putting in the work. I'll be completely honest, adventure sports podcast feels that way some days. There's lots of stagnant minor growth over a long period of time with lots of unexpected jumps here and there. For whatever reason, it gets shared somewhere or it gets featured in something or you have a conversation you just can't believe you just had in a good way. And it is that that just keeps you going some days.\n\nCole Heilborn (24:09): So it sounds like though, for you, in order to recognize those opportunities, you have to be alert and you have to be looking for them because you could just miss them.", "token_estimate": 530, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Mason Gravely"], "start_timestamp": "22:02", "end_timestamp": "24:09", "podcast_index": 93, "episode_name": "Ep 93: Finding the Balance Between Vision and Expectations | Mason Gravley | Athletic Brewing", "slug": "ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "How do you find a balance between vision and expectations ?So often in today's culture, we're told to set a goal and work tirelessly to it - sometimes to a fault.", "podcast_length": "44:115"}, {"id": "2a26facb-8ea2-443c-8d14-b4c65fa15995", "episode_id": "704a67f4-7ebe-4752-b628-13b12c4efe4d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4885", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Mason Gravely (24:19): You can. And that's terrifying to think of how many opportunities you potentially have missed. It's like exploring a cave, you know what I mean? The entries are not obvious. It could be a pinhole that could open up to a grand room that you kind of have to be watching for. It's not going to be a doorway necessarily in a natural cave that opens up amazing opportunity to you. It might be something incredibly tiny. And if you talk to anybody that explores caves, that's exactly what it is a lot of the times, and I think life is a lot of times the same way, but you do get better at recognizing opportunity the more you think like that and the more you just be willing to say, yes, my wife and I or my partner, she is way less like that, way less like this. She wants to find her groove, find her purpose and find something she really enjoys and that's it. (25:19): That's what she wants to do the same way every day. Loves it. So thankfully, she's really set up now. She's got jobs she wants and the commute and all that, and she'd be happy if that never changes for the rest of her life, which I find crazy versus me. It's like it's just the way I'm motivated. But if that is your life right now, but you want it to be different, there's a billion opportunities she comes across every day if she chose to look at it that way. And that's all it is, is choosing to look at it that way. Richard Branson has a really good kind of mindset that I like is opportunity is like a bus stop. Don't freak out if you miss that bus because there's going to be another bus coming. There's always another bus. The cadence of the bus might be different depending on where you are and there's ways you can increase it, but find a bus stop and just watch for the bus. (26:19): It's pretty obvious when it comes. A lot of the times they're pretty huge and loud, just get on and you only need to get on one. You know what I mean? You don't have to. So I've definitely learned to not, especially now with kids and things where life just, there's certain things I can't say yes to anymore, at least not as frequently. I get to say yes to a lot of things, still most things, but sometimes I'm like, there's more opportunity out there. I don't need to be obsessive about turning over every rock, but it does keep you on your toes because you never know where life's going to take. And if that excites you, that's definitely the way I enjoy living.", "token_estimate": 612, "speakers": ["Mason Gravely"], "start_timestamp": "24:19", "end_timestamp": "24:19", "podcast_index": 93, "episode_name": "Ep 93: Finding the Balance Between Vision and Expectations | Mason Gravley | Athletic Brewing", "slug": "ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "How do you find a balance between vision and expectations ?So often in today's culture, we're told to set a goal and work tirelessly to it - sometimes to a fault.", "podcast_length": "44:115"}, {"id": "d93b8975-60e6-400c-9ed7-bd81acf1328d", "episode_id": "704a67f4-7ebe-4752-b628-13b12c4efe4d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4885", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Cole Heilborn (27:02): The more we talk, the more I think about society's pressure or encouragement for people just to go out and chase their dreams and stop at nothing to get to them. I'm realizing what you're saying isn't exactly the opposite of that, but what's your take on this idea of chasing dreams at every cost or no cost?\n\nMason Gravely (27:25): Yeah, you're helping me formalize my philosophy as we talk about it. I don't know if a lot of people spend their time really thinking through it like this, and I don't know if I ever have to this degree. So I appreciate this opportunity. This is really cool. This is off the cuff. I haven't really thought about this, but just for that question, I work for people and I have know plenty of people that are driven by one single goal. And if you know what that is and you feel strongly about it and you really believe in it, fantastic, absolutely go for it. But I've learned that one size does not fit all with how we live this life. There's a billion different ways set. Well close to 8 billion ways to live this life, and a lot of them are awesome. And so I would say if you are someone that's driven to find one single goal, go for it. (28:26): If you're not like me and you've been frustrated because you felt like you've had to, you've needed to think that way to succeed. Let that go as soon as you can because find out what really does motivate you and what your values are. There's plenty I've learned through my life in the last few years. Wow. It really doesn't matter what I do for a living. It is kind of who it's for and what kind of impact I'm making. And so there are some dreams and goals I've had to let go recently. I'm not going to be that building trails all the time, for instance. But through athletic Bruin, 2% of all our sales goes to building trails. So in a way I am able to make more of an impact with the things I actually do enjoy by focusing on my job. There's all kinds of little things I've learned like that throughout the last few years of, like Seth said, not holding on too tightly to a specific dream, but chasing opportunity as it comes on your way, on your pursuit to that dream in the long run. So yeah, I don't know. I'm also intensely curious person, so all sorts of subjects interest me, but if you have that one single goal, I know plenty of people that do, they are passionate about this thing. I would just be sure that you're not driving yourself crazy or ruining that passion for you by trying to find that path that just isn't there.", "token_estimate": 634, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Mason Gravely"], "start_timestamp": "27:02", "end_timestamp": "27:25", "podcast_index": 93, "episode_name": "Ep 93: Finding the Balance Between Vision and Expectations | Mason Gravley | Athletic Brewing", "slug": "ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "How do you find a balance between vision and expectations ?So often in today's culture, we're told to set a goal and work tirelessly to it - sometimes to a fault.", "podcast_length": "44:115"}, {"id": "8eddbc6e-85aa-4114-9c4f-194429434a80", "episode_id": "704a67f4-7ebe-4752-b628-13b12c4efe4d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4885", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Cole Heilborn (30:05): Was there ever a point where you felt like you needed to have that beacon that you were working towards that one goal? And when you realized that that's not the way that you wanted to live your life, was that a hard transition? I think a lot of people feel like they need to have something that they're striving towards, but they don't. They take this go with the flow mentality, but because of whatever internal or external pressure they feel like they have to be working towards this monument.", "token_estimate": 130, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "30:05", "end_timestamp": "30:05", "podcast_index": 93, "episode_name": "Ep 93: Finding the Balance Between Vision and Expectations | Mason Gravley | Athletic Brewing", "slug": "ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "How do you find a balance between vision and expectations ?So often in today's culture, we're told to set a goal and work tirelessly to it - sometimes to a fault.", "podcast_length": "44:115"}, {"id": "82080b90-2b71-49f8-b9be-66cff68495c0", "episode_id": "704a67f4-7ebe-4752-b628-13b12c4efe4d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4885", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Mason Gravely (30:33): That's interesting. I feel like I'm thinking about this all the time. I guess I've just never been one to have that long-term goal. When I started playing basketball, for instance, it was like, all right, get to college, make a college team, but beyond. And then I got to college and I'm like, wow, I don't really like this. It's way harder in college than it's like a job now. And so I pursued a different passion, which was biology, and that would clearly lead someone to explore the world. And so when I discovered that, I discovered bike packing and bike packing took over just because of the ability to explore. And then that led to all sorts of things. Now, I guess that's one reason the Adventure Sports podcast is so varied. I've wanted to take sports out of the title for a while and just say the Adventure Stories podcast, because that's all it is. (31:27): It's stories of adventure because that's what I've learned to love the story aspect. So to answer your question about having that pressure to do so, to have some sort of single goal, I do feel at times society does push on us to grow. And I'll be honest, I think I'm fighting back on that a little bit personally this year. Something I try to have an overarch. Now I will say this, I try to have an over overarching theme of the year, something just to keep in a filter, to think every thought through. And I'm writing this on my desk or anything. I probably should, honestly, actually that's a really good thing to do. I should write it right here just to keep it in mind, but I'm not getting a tattoo over anything. Last year it was like literally have fun, have more fun, quit being such a tight, just so uptight all the time. (32:29): The year before that was just gratitude. No, not gratitude, generosity. Just be more generous. I'm a stingy person. Stingy, I'm really cheap. And so my wife was like, you need to just give more money away. And so I really focused on that two years ago and now I feel like it has stuck with me to where it's like, and being at a company, this is not a salesy thing, but we're very generous at athletic and we just give a lot of money away. And it's given me the freedom personally to do the same thing. And it's really fun. Last year was just have more fun this year is being more content with where things are instead of searching for that singular purpose. And I think you get lost in, or I'm learning from other people telling me that I'm getting really frustrated with not having a purpose. (33:23): I felt like I haven't had that North star in a while if I have ever really had it. And so this year I'm actually taking even a further step back from that and saying, all right, focus on being content first. I believe that North Star will come because have you known anybody that wants something so bad that they pretty much ruin their opportunity to have it? Whether that be a partner or a certain career path that we're talking about. Someone wants something so bad that it's so obsessive that when they get the opportunity, they ruin it like a turnoff. You know what I mean? Or it's like, whoa, try hard or whatnot. I feel like can be the same thing with contentment or happiness or whatever. You almost have to shoot beyond things or around things to get them in return. There's all kinds of Corning quotes, like shoot for the stars and you land shoot for the moon, you'll land among the stars, which is totally stupid. Like stars are so far away. The moon's right there. Anyway,", "token_estimate": 879, "speakers": ["Mason Gravely"], "start_timestamp": "30:33", "end_timestamp": "30:33", "podcast_index": 93, "episode_name": "Ep 93: Finding the Balance Between Vision and Expectations | Mason Gravley | Athletic Brewing", "slug": "ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "How do you find a balance between vision and expectations ?So often in today's culture, we're told to set a goal and work tirelessly to it - sometimes to a fault.", "podcast_length": "44:115"}, {"id": "9e42b43a-7f06-4e27-973f-fd7e146396f7", "episode_id": "704a67f4-7ebe-4752-b628-13b12c4efe4d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4885", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Cole Heilborn (34:29): I'd be happy with the moon.\n\nMason Gravely (34:30): Yeah, the moon's great. I'm like, you can't even be on a star. You can at least be on the moon with funny thing, super random side tour story, but something that got me super excited just the other day. That's a total opportunity that I created out of nowhere. If you want to hear it. I know this is a little bit of a tangent.\n\nCole Heilborn (34:53): Yeah,\n\nMason Gravely (34:53): Go for it. But it has to do with the moon, and it's something that if this is the kind of thing I love,\n\nCole Heilborn (34:57): Are you going to become one of the next astronauts?\n\nMason Gravely (34:59): Oh gosh, no. No. Unless no, absolutely not. I would never, well, we'll see.\n\nCole Heilborn (35:07): Go with the flow.", "token_estimate": 195, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Mason Gravely"], "start_timestamp": "34:29", "end_timestamp": "35:07", "podcast_index": 93, "episode_name": "Ep 93: Finding the Balance Between Vision and Expectations | Mason Gravley | Athletic Brewing", "slug": "ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "How do you find a balance between vision and expectations ?So often in today's culture, we're told to set a goal and work tirelessly to it - sometimes to a fault.", "podcast_length": "44:115"}, {"id": "c7307e12-a4eb-4753-9f8d-be1c8360d4f0", "episode_id": "704a67f4-7ebe-4752-b628-13b12c4efe4d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4885", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Mason Gravely (35:08): Go with the flow. If the flow goes to the moon, I'll go to the moon. But I was at a museum by my house, and it's a super small town, nothing major, and it's a very small local museum, and I saw a piece of the moon there. It was in the museum on display. I was like, I don't know much about this, but it feels rare to have a sizable piece of the moon here at our little museum. And it looked like a new display. I was inquiring about it. No one really seemed to, I don't know, think that much of it. I was like, I don't know how rare it is to have a pretty big chunk of the moon that I don't think we have that much of the moon here on earth that we've harvested from the Apollo emissions. But I forgot about it. (35:53): The day goes on. By the time we got home, I just didn't think about it. Two weeks later, I'm listening to a podcast on Moon Rocks and I'm like, this is interesting. And it was talking about how the moon rocks were gifted to all the countries in the EU or the European Union. Everyone got one. All the 50 states got a piece of the moon, but the podcast mentioned the pieces that are the size of a couple grains of rice was the gift size. And that got me thinking again, I'm like, wait, that size, the piece I saw was much larger than that. Not huge, but a pretty big piece. And we only have, I don't know, less, I think less than a thousand pounds of the moon on earth. So obviously tons of it. You need to be used for research and whatnot. Anyway, I started doing some research. (36:41): I'm like, well, why do we have such a big piece? And come to find out, one of the astronauts that landed on the moon, there was 12 over the Apollo missions. One of them lived in this small town, and when he passed away, he didn't originally live here, but his family moved here after he passed away. And with him, they brought a lot of his memorabilia and they were looking for a place to donate it. And so they donated to our museum. So I googled his former wife, his widow is still alive, she's in her eighties, and I just googled her name and I found a phone number and I gave it a call and she answered. And we talked for an hour about her husband, his career on the moon, what it was like to be married to someone with kids while you're like, what was it like? (37:32): She goes, well, we had to rent a tv, first of all to watch him on the moon. And I remember I was cooking dinner, this is the sixties. Things were definitely stereotypical back then and a lot of families. And she goes, I remember getting dinner ready and I had a lot of the family over it, and my kids kept trying to change the channel to cartoons. We never had a TV in our house, we just rented it for the event. And I was like, no, your dad's on the moon. We got to turn it back. And I was like, my mind was blown. And come to find out, she lives like 10 minutes down the road from me, so I'm going to go over her house and she's going to show me all this memorabilia, show me a spacesuit. And she's got more chunks of the moon there. (38:12): And she's like, bring your kids. They'd love it. And I said, how random is that? But it just started with this curiosity about one thing and just putting pieces together. That's all it is. It's being curious and being alert. And that literally, that could be the total end of that story is just having that one conversation with the widower or the, I'm sorry, the widow of one of the moonwalks. But to me that was just a cool thing to talk about and a cool thing to experience. And she was a total hoot, just was making jokes and totally not that old yet. So it was really fun conversation. And so who knows where that leads, but to me that's just really exciting to do things like that. So personally, I'm always trying to do just something like that, or at least just being alert for when things crossover like that. That was a long story, but that was just an example.", "token_estimate": 981, "speakers": ["Mason Gravely"], "start_timestamp": "35:08", "end_timestamp": "35:08", "podcast_index": 93, "episode_name": "Ep 93: Finding the Balance Between Vision and Expectations | Mason Gravley | Athletic Brewing", "slug": "ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "How do you find a balance between vision and expectations ?So often in today's culture, we're told to set a goal and work tirelessly to it - sometimes to a fault.", "podcast_length": "44:115"}, {"id": "edff98e7-978c-4b4f-9b15-8c812fe0fb6f", "episode_id": "704a67f4-7ebe-4752-b628-13b12c4efe4d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4885", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Cole Heilborn (39:16): That's an amazing story. I want to hear the follow-up calls and conversations you have with this woman.\n\nMason Gravely (39:23): I told her I wanted to do a podcast episode with her, which we're planning, and she's like, I would absolutely love to. And I said, sweet. Well, it really is just a matter of going over there and recording some stories. So I would love to keep you posted when that happens, but if that's the extent of it, that's the kind of stuff I'm happy doing.\n\nCole Heilborn (39:44): Lately I've been thinking about the concept of seasons, and here in the northwest we have I'd say maybe two and a half or three seasons. And we're currently, we just got out of, well, we actually had a bunch of snow up here. That's pretty uncommon for us at least in terms of the amount of quantity and how long it lasted. Anyways, I was thinking about seasons when we had this snow and thinking about how the world around us constantly changes. And seasons are such a great example of that. And in different seasons of the year, nature responds a different way. In the wintertime, everything slows down. Life doesn't grow as quickly. Everything kind of hibernates. And I promise this will connect, but I've been thinking about, we were also thinking about our podcast, and as we've jumped into season three with this new year, we've been working on an annual, we put out an episode every Wednesday for the entire year, and we reached a point, I dunno, last fall, where we were like, do we want to be doing this every day for the entire year? Could we adopt more of a seasonality approach? And so I guess my thought process is if the natural world has these cycles of life and growth and slowness, why do we feel the need all the time to be pushing forwards, constantly driving forwards when everything around us ebbs and flows?", "token_estimate": 463, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Mason Gravely"], "start_timestamp": "39:16", "end_timestamp": "39:44", "podcast_index": 93, "episode_name": "Ep 93: Finding the Balance Between Vision and Expectations | Mason Gravley | Athletic Brewing", "slug": "ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "How do you find a balance between vision and expectations ?So often in today's culture, we're told to set a goal and work tirelessly to it - sometimes to a fault.", "podcast_length": "44:115"}, {"id": "d71ecc85-6e59-4170-83b5-b63d32d1320b", "episode_id": "704a67f4-7ebe-4752-b628-13b12c4efe4d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4885", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Mason Gravely (41:14): That's a great question. Yeah, I'm just riffing on this right now, but I love learning from the natural world. I'd say a lot of the principles I live by are natural concepts that you learn just by taking a hike or walking out in the woods or on the beach or whatever. I think there's so much we can gain from the natural world, and I think a lot of our drives now are evolutionarily based, the drive to grow and pursue, and maybe that's an exchange of some sort from the drive to procreate on a constant basis, which obviously isn't socially acceptable anymore to the degree it used to be have tens of thousands of years ago. Or for a dog for instance, my dog or anything, any other animal. So I feel like we do, based on society, have shifted our desires and growth is clearly one that we feel instinctively that we have to. (42:21): I fight against that with the show. I'm talking with the folks, someone that helps me with the podcast and it's like, what if we just didn't worry about growing this year? What if we didn't set goals? What if we just did the show and had fun and enjoyed it? Is that so freaking wrong? It's not like it's making a million dollars anyway. Why not just have a good time? And I think it was a little bit of a realization like, whoa, I guess that is an option, but that feels wrong. I'm not exactly sure why that feels wrong, but I'm also not someone that tries to summit every mountain they see. I really enjoy just sauntering around through the woods and looking at things. And so I would say if you don't feel like setting those big goals and you are content with things being smaller, you have a much higher chance of success. (43:21): I was introduced through the outdoors. I always mention this through hunting and fishing down here in the south where I live. And when I first started doing that, I felt like the only way to experience the outdoors was hunting and fishing, which was you killing something or catching something and releasing it. And at some point I was like, I was terrible at it. My dad was, I love him to death, but we weren't very good at either one. And so I said, I'm just going to stop setting this goal every time I come out here just because everyone else sets that goal. I don't have to, I kind of just enjoy being out here. And so I remember when I became an adult, especially just realizing without really knowing much about the rest of the outdoor adventure culture, that you don't have to go out there and kill something. (44:13): And when I took that goal off the table, there's a host of reasons why I got rid of that goal, but now I feel much more successful every time I go out side because I go out with the idea that I want a paddleboard today or I want to bike 20 miles or just go for a stroll or hike up that ridge and come back down. I feel much more complete and successful. So it's not like, Hey, set your goals super low and you can exceed, like set your goals where it's going to give you the most fulfillment. And if that's climbing Mount Everest, that's climbing Mount Everest, if that's just going for a walk every day in your neighborhood, then that's what it is for you. So they're all meaningful if that's what you want to do. But as far as how that translates into our careers and whatnot, yeah, man, I don't know. I'm still figuring all this out too.", "token_estimate": 840, "speakers": ["Mason Gravely"], "start_timestamp": "41:14", "end_timestamp": "41:14", "podcast_index": 93, "episode_name": "Ep 93: Finding the Balance Between Vision and Expectations | Mason Gravley | Athletic Brewing", "slug": "ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "How do you find a balance between vision and expectations ?So often in today's culture, we're told to set a goal and work tirelessly to it - sometimes to a fault.", "podcast_length": "44:115"}, {"id": "b49bfafb-66c8-4dbe-a88c-a37966e0d363", "episode_id": "704a67f4-7ebe-4752-b628-13b12c4efe4d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4885", "chunk_index": 19, "content": "Cole Heilborn (45:12): And I think everyone comes to the table with their own set of their own world and their own worldly expectations that they have with them, whether they've been brought up with these expectations or just their culture has imposed these expectations. And I think the idea of managing your own expectations is also really interesting. That's what we've been discussing about today, but making your own expectations and owning them for what your values are rather than just accepting whatever expectations are placed on you, I think is easy to say, but very difficult to do.\n\nMason Gravely (45:48): It is, sure, a lot of us have jobs that demand a lot of us or families whatnot. I know with work we're pushing, we're pushing to be a global brand and that's exciting, that's fun. But you can have, and also I love you can have different buckets of your life too. Just because I'm in completely driven with my career, which I am and I want to be, I don't have to translate that to my family or to my own hobbies or side hustles, whatever you want to call 'em or interests. And I love having buckets in life like that where you can kind of experience certain aspects of maybe you're not this way on a flat line natural level, but you get to express that here. You get to be something else and try new things. But when I'm me, it is definitely much more laid back than usually when I'm got my game phase on.\n\nCole Heilborn (46:54): Yeah, that's a great point. You can create areas of your life where you have different levels of, I don't know, focus or drive, and it doesn't have to be even across all of the aspects of your life. Mason, I want to thank you for taking the time to chat. We're kind of coming up against the end of our time for today. Do you have any other lasting thoughts that you want to leave our audience with? I know we've kind of gone to the moon and back a little bit here.", "token_estimate": 478, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Mason Gravely"], "start_timestamp": "45:12", "end_timestamp": "46:54", "podcast_index": 93, "episode_name": "Ep 93: Finding the Balance Between Vision and Expectations | Mason Gravley | Athletic Brewing", "slug": "ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "How do you find a balance between vision and expectations ?So often in today's culture, we're told to set a goal and work tirelessly to it - sometimes to a fault.", "podcast_length": "44:115"}, {"id": "1ed1de43-ca05-4444-960f-d458971b04af", "episode_id": "704a67f4-7ebe-4752-b628-13b12c4efe4d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4885", "chunk_index": 20, "content": "Mason Gravely (47:20): Yeah, I don't know what the common theme is. I think it's just see where the path takes you. If you enjoy being out there on the path, great. And don't be afraid of just doing things differently. I'll say that's probably one of the biggest things that's led to any success I have, which probably pretty not necessarily spectacular success, but I'm happy is just being willing to do things a little bit differently, take a few risks and clearly try new things. But if you're the type of person that likes to go with the flow, if you're a type B person in a type A world, there is still place for you and anything you want to do. So just keep going. So I think that's probably the most important thing with anything I tell people is just keep going. Like you said, with your own career, you never know when that break will be. (48:20): If that break never comes, make sure it's a pace that's sustainable for you. Make sure you as a river are flowing at a pace that you can control. There will be times there's rapids and there will be times that you're in a lake trying to make it to the other side. It's very calm. There'll be times where it just flows really well, really nice. And there'll be times crash into a chute to go uphill and it's just not going to work. So you turn around and you go back down and that's fine. Test all that out and you're going to find your path. And that doesn't mean you're not working hard and you're not doing things that you're passionate about, but you just got to keep going. It's our nature to just keep going, so don't fight that and keep going.\n\nCole Heilborn (49:02): Yeah, well said. If folks want to follow along with you on any of your, I dunno, channels, where can people go to connect?", "token_estimate": 439, "speakers": ["Mason Gravely", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "47:20", "end_timestamp": "49:02", "podcast_index": 93, "episode_name": "Ep 93: Finding the Balance Between Vision and Expectations | Mason Gravley | Athletic Brewing", "slug": "ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "How do you find a balance between vision and expectations ?So often in today's culture, we're told to set a goal and work tirelessly to it - sometimes to a fault.", "podcast_length": "44:115"}, {"id": "b714674a-69a5-4647-83b2-77f3e1722825", "episode_id": "704a67f4-7ebe-4752-b628-13b12c4efe4d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4885", "chunk_index": 21, "content": "Mason Gravely (49:12): Adventure Sports podcast? You find us anywhere. We got a website that.com, adventure sports podcast.com, and then athletic brewing. If you're thinking, huh, we didn't talk a lot about your weirdo non-alcoholic beer company, definitely give it a shot. I'd be happy to mail anybody beer that was interested in trying it. We make non-alcoholic craft beer, it's a pretty crazy idea. And so I say this all the time, it's like, Hey, I sell non-alcoholic beer for a living. Everything else sounds way more possible and doable and almost normal in comparison. So it's a crazy thing, but it's quite literally taking over. So it's pretty cool to see. And we're everywhere. We're all over the Pacific Northwest and pretty much anywhere you can get beer, you can pretty much get us now.\n\nCole Heilborn (50:07): Right on. Well, I enjoy all the conversations we have on the show, but this one in particular, because work is great and talking about work, very specific work things is fun, but sometimes it's nice to look at work and life from a different perspective. And so I feel like Mason, that's kind of what this conversation has been and I'm sure everyone will resonate with it differently. So I want to thank you for your time and keep us posted on this Moon Story podcast that's coming out. Eventually.\n\nMason Gravely (50:36): We'll see where that goes. Alright, thank you.\n\nCole Heilborn (50:38): Yeah, thank you and have a good day.\n\nMason Gravely (50:40): You too.\n\nCole Heilborn (50:43): Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learned something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 430, "speakers": ["Mason Gravely", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "49:12", "end_timestamp": "50:43", "podcast_index": 93, "episode_name": "Ep 93: Finding the Balance Between Vision and Expectations | Mason Gravley | Athletic Brewing", "slug": "ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-93-finding-the-balance-between-vision-and-expectations-mason-gravley-athletic-brewing", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "How do you find a balance between vision and expectations ?So often in today's culture, we're told to set a goal and work tirelessly to it - sometimes to a fault.", "podcast_length": "44:115"}, {"id": "74b785e2-2c50-4dec-ab0f-8c087d37f313", "episode_id": "ffbbb1e5-59f1-4a24-84d5-2d9d6d31b3e1", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4886", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Jon Glassberg (00:03): All of those things have so many layers to pre-production, production, and post-production that go into creating something decent. And you start to realize that as a freelancer, that you can't just do it all yourself. You have to focus on what you're good at and really kind of dive into that. Spreading yourself thin. You'll be average at all those things all at once when you're trying to pull it off. But if you can focus, you can be really, really good at one specific thing, and that's when you become really valuable in my opinion.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:33): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing podcast. My name is Cool Hilborn, and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam, or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Back Country Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Portside Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Heur. Today I'm sitting down with John Glassberg. He's a director, photographer, a professional climber, and he runs louder than 11. John, how are you?\n\nJon Glassberg (01:18): Great. Thanks for having me on the show. I'm super honored.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:21): I'm honored as well. I'm looking forward to our conversation today. I'm looking forward to getting to know you a little bit more and kind of diving into our topic for the day. How's your week going?\n\nJon Glassberg (01:30): It's going well, just getting back in the swing of things after the new year, and I don't know, nothing really happens in the outdoor industry between Thanksgiving and New Year. So suddenly the flood gates are open and it's back to work as usual with everybody expecting things quickly and right two days ago, and all the normal stuff that you're used to in the scene. So yeah, back at it.", "token_estimate": 523, "speakers": ["Jon Glassberg", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:03", "end_timestamp": "01:30", "podcast_index": 94, "episode_name": "Ep 94: Finding Your Focus As A Freelancer | Jon Glassberg | Louder Than 11", "slug": "ep-94-finding-your-focus-as-a-freelancer-jon-glassberg-louder-than-11", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-94-finding-your-focus-as-a-freelancer-jon-glassberg-louder-than-11", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Freelancer Month! This whole month, we'll be sharing episodes that are geared more towards freelancers and answering the top questions they may have.", "podcast_length": "44:116"}, {"id": "a3376ed8-525c-456a-b1bc-dbee8bccf61a", "episode_id": "ffbbb1e5-59f1-4a24-84d5-2d9d6d31b3e1", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4886", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Cole Heilborn (01:53): Nice. Well, as you know, we're recording this episode as part of our freelance focused month where we're focusing the entire show for the month of March on topics related strictly to freelancing being a creative in the outdoor industry. John, we've had a chance to connect prior to this call and kind of set up what we're going to be discussing today, and we're going to get deep into the weeds in some of this stuff here. But our topic for today is the idea of niching and choosing a focus as a creative. So I'm excited to dig into that. But before we do, we've got a quick segment that we've been doing at the start of every episode that we've recorded in the new year, and we're calling it the quickfire segment. It's a few questions I'm going to ask 'em quickly, and then you're going to respond quickly. And so hopefully this gives people a good idea, a little bit more about you. So number one, where are you located?\n\nJon Glassberg (02:43): Boulder, Colorado. I'm in my office right now.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:45): Okay. What is the best climbing spot off the radar that people don't know about?\n\nJon Glassberg (02:51): Boone, North Carolina.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:53): Okay. And I know you might have a long answer for this, but if you could condense the answer, what's the wildest expedition you've been on?\n\nJon Glassberg (03:02): Let's see. I went on a crazy expedition to Peru a couple of years ago with Emily Harrington and a good friend of hers, and we went on a big wall expedition, essentially, and I was super sick the entire time. I probably had COVID before it was COVID. And yeah, it was just a survival mission because we were so sick at Basecamp at 15,000 feet climbing to 18, almost 19,000 feet, so we were just destroyed. It was very multiple.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:34): Is that project done?\n\nJon Glassberg (03:36): Yes, it's done. It was a piece for Red Bull with Michelle Parker. She's a free skier and good friends of Emily. And Emily was kind of trading skiing with Michelle for Michelle trading, skiing with, or big wall climbing with Emily, and they went to Peru and Emily kind of helped her, guided her up this big wall, which was super fun.", "token_estimate": 547, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Jon Glassberg"], "start_timestamp": "01:53", "end_timestamp": "03:36", "podcast_index": 94, "episode_name": "Ep 94: Finding Your Focus As A Freelancer | Jon Glassberg | Louder Than 11", "slug": "ep-94-finding-your-focus-as-a-freelancer-jon-glassberg-louder-than-11", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-94-finding-your-focus-as-a-freelancer-jon-glassberg-louder-than-11", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Freelancer Month! This whole month, we'll be sharing episodes that are geared more towards freelancers and answering the top questions they may have.", "podcast_length": "44:116"}, {"id": "1076f716-9b0c-4d24-a782-e362136c681a", "episode_id": "ffbbb1e5-59f1-4a24-84d5-2d9d6d31b3e1", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4886", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Cole Heilborn (03:56): Oh, cool. And last question, who's your hero?\n\nJon Glassberg (03:59): Oh, man. I don't know that I have a single hero\n\nCole Heilborn (04:06): Or a collective or a pantheon of heroes.\n\nJon Glassberg (04:10): I really like just individual's work, whether it's in the outdoor industry or in film or in photography in general. Let's see, who are my heroes? Geez, one of my good friends is Keith Lozinsky, and he is extremely passionate about working all day, every day in the outdoor space, and I think his work ethic is super impressive. So he is definitely a personal hero within my bubble. I also, I don't know, I'm just really inspired by film directors and stuff, so I've always looked up to Ron Howard and let's see, who else? Yeah, I dunno. That's a good starting point.\n\nCole Heilborn (04:54): Alright, perfect, perfect. Well, John, I'd love that you could just give us a quick introduction about yourself and louder than 11, what you guys do. We're talking about niching and focusing, and for folks who are familiar, they'll know that you guys produce some pretty amazing rock climbing films, photos, everything. So I'll let you take it away and give us an introduction.", "token_estimate": 300, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Jon Glassberg"], "start_timestamp": "03:56", "end_timestamp": "04:54", "podcast_index": 94, "episode_name": "Ep 94: Finding Your Focus As A Freelancer | Jon Glassberg | Louder Than 11", "slug": "ep-94-finding-your-focus-as-a-freelancer-jon-glassberg-louder-than-11", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-94-finding-your-focus-as-a-freelancer-jon-glassberg-louder-than-11", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Freelancer Month! This whole month, we'll be sharing episodes that are geared more towards freelancers and answering the top questions they may have.", "podcast_length": "44:116"}, {"id": "d5bec042-89d8-4bd8-b053-26e88fa47670", "episode_id": "ffbbb1e5-59f1-4a24-84d5-2d9d6d31b3e1", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4886", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Jon Glassberg (05:15): Yeah, so I'm 37 now. I started climbing when I was 11 in Charlottesville, Virginia. That's where I grew up. And I took climbing very seriously through middle school and high school, and then I took a year off after high school. I didn't know what I wanted to do and I kind of just wanted to climb. So I moved to Boone in North Carolina to explore climbing and what that meant, and I didn't really know where it would take me, but I knew that that's all I wanted to do and that's all that mattered. I spent a year in Boone. I was doing all kinds of part-time jobs and just trying to scrape to get by, but to be a climber as much as I could be. And I eventually ended up going to school at App State. I did undergrad and grad school there, and I studied graphic design and business and a little bit of photography and print media actually was my main focus. So I spent a lot of time in that world, various jobs of different types, but nothing very serious and still climbing semi-professionally, professionally throughout that whole time. And then I linked up with a friend in North Carolina and we got really passionate about making climbing films in college. And that was kind of around the time that the DSLR came out. And then suddenly pretty high-end video and video capabilities were available in very inexpensive cameras.\n\nCole Heilborn (06:53): Was that with the Canon 70 D?", "token_estimate": 351, "speakers": ["Jon Glassberg", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "05:15", "end_timestamp": "06:53", "podcast_index": 94, "episode_name": "Ep 94: Finding Your Focus As A Freelancer | Jon Glassberg | Louder Than 11", "slug": "ep-94-finding-your-focus-as-a-freelancer-jon-glassberg-louder-than-11", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-94-finding-your-focus-as-a-freelancer-jon-glassberg-louder-than-11", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Freelancer Month! This whole month, we'll be sharing episodes that are geared more towards freelancers and answering the top questions they may have.", "podcast_length": "44:116"}, {"id": "a2e9712a-52cd-4f06-84c7-b2285ca35ee8", "episode_id": "ffbbb1e5-59f1-4a24-84d5-2d9d6d31b3e1", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4886", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Jon Glassberg (06:55): Oh yeah. I think I had a six D before that. And then some other maybe 70 D or something really original DSLR. I mean, it was total junk and the videos we were making were horrible, but it was super fun to learn through that process. And I was friends with a lot of climbers and we would travel all over the world kind of documenting what we were doing and turning around those edits within a few days and just kind of cranking out media. I mean, we were making a couple hundred videos every year on different individual boulder problems that we were climbing or sport climbs or trips or whatever it might be. So over the years of doing that with the company louder than 11 that I started, it snowballed and took off and we were kind of on the forefront of internet web free content, basically. (07:50): I mean, we were just content creators basically before we knew what it was. And we were just making tons and tons of climbing films and we didn't care if we got paid for it, we just were pumping 'em out. And we kind of developed our relationships within the industry because of that. And people knew who we were because we were extremely core and we were just creating climbing films all day every day. And eventually as we kind of got better and better at making films, I really was honing my skills as an editor, as a filmmaker, as a director, we started getting more production jobs and we started doing more commercial stuff. And one thing led to another, and that's kind of where we're at now. We do a ton of commercial things, but we also capture lots of documentary and expedition film. And yeah, it's kind of morphing between a DP and a director that connects kind of the outdoor space to a commercial space because that's becoming more and more valuable to people these days, especially with huge films hitting the mainstream about rock climbing and expeditions. (09:04): So it's kind of changed the whole narrative around what the commercial and agency world is comfortable with. So that's kind of taken off, but I still pursue a professional climbing career. I had my best climbing year of my whole life last year. So I mean, certainly haven't slowed down in that regard at all. And I've just gotten smarter about how to position myself as a pro athlete and where my value is in the industry and that kind of thing. But I consider myself to have three jobs, essentially three businesses. One would be louder than 11, my photo and video production company. The second would be my professional climbing career. And the third is this company called Crux Academy that I co-founded with a partner, Jeremy Jensen, who I think you've had on the podcast. Yeah, so that's kind of like the full-time gig is those three jobs. Plus being married and trying to have a life outside of film and photo production. It's a lot.", "token_estimate": 712, "speakers": ["Jon Glassberg"], "start_timestamp": "06:55", "end_timestamp": "06:55", "podcast_index": 94, "episode_name": "Ep 94: Finding Your Focus As A Freelancer | Jon Glassberg | Louder Than 11", "slug": "ep-94-finding-your-focus-as-a-freelancer-jon-glassberg-louder-than-11", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-94-finding-your-focus-as-a-freelancer-jon-glassberg-louder-than-11", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Freelancer Month! This whole month, we'll be sharing episodes that are geared more towards freelancers and answering the top questions they may have.", "podcast_length": "44:116"}, {"id": "d3670426-9bd6-407e-bd8b-f905f54ab615", "episode_id": "ffbbb1e5-59f1-4a24-84d5-2d9d6d31b3e1", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4886", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Cole Heilborn (10:11): What are some of the projects that you guys have produced? I know recently, or maybe not recently, but in the past you shot the masterclass that featured Alex Hod and was it Tommy Caldwell?\n\nJon Glassberg (10:23): Yep. Yeah, I worked on that masterclass project, which was in May of 2021, which came out in I think September, October of 21. And yeah, that was pretty cool. That was full blown Hollywood meets the outdoor industry on everybody.\n\nCole Heilborn (10:40): You had crafty taking care of you.\n\nJon Glassberg (10:42): Oh yeah. I mean, we shot in this tiny little LA climbing gym for a few days, and there was probably 150 people on the set. Everybody had their own trailers and stuff. It was everything you would imagine to happen to Brad Pitt or Tom Cruise or something on a movie set, but it was Alex Ho and Tommy Caldwell. They thought it was pretty funny too. So I think overall it was good because we got to hang with our friends and make something about climbing for the mainstream to see, which was pretty cool. But for me, it was exciting. It was like a union job that showed me a lot more of what's in the Hollywood space and how big it is and crazy it is, but also why it works so well. It's like a well-oiled machine and it works for a reason and you get results, which is pretty cool. (11:34): And then we also did a shoot outside. We shot a few days in Red Rock in Las Vegas, and that was pretty hilarious because as you can imagine, the Hollywood scene doesn't really do a lot of location climbing work, so that was very exciting. And I dpd the whole climbing section, so it was very spicy with some of the Hollywood guys cruising around in the desert, getting poked by cactuses, and we were shooting in a hundred degree weather, and it was pretty rugged actually. So they got a good taste of what it's like to be on expedition.\n\nCole Heilborn (12:11): Those two worlds are a little different, the expedition style adventure filmmaker versus the Hollywood climate controlled studio set. And not that that world is bad, but they are very different when it comes to production work.", "token_estimate": 532, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Jon Glassberg"], "start_timestamp": "10:11", "end_timestamp": "12:11", "podcast_index": 94, "episode_name": "Ep 94: Finding Your Focus As A Freelancer | Jon Glassberg | Louder Than 11", "slug": "ep-94-finding-your-focus-as-a-freelancer-jon-glassberg-louder-than-11", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-94-finding-your-focus-as-a-freelancer-jon-glassberg-louder-than-11", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Freelancer Month! This whole month, we'll be sharing episodes that are geared more towards freelancers and answering the top questions they may have.", "podcast_length": "44:116"}, {"id": "1589fa00-9101-4844-a9bd-466daa650f99", "episode_id": "ffbbb1e5-59f1-4a24-84d5-2d9d6d31b3e1", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4886", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Jon Glassberg (12:26): They just take it to the extreme, which is cool. I mean, I totally appreciate it. The assistant camera guys and girls that were out supporting, they're not used to shooting on locations, but there's a whole team that is just designed to make everybody safe and make sure they can get to the spot they want to be in, and then building stuff on location to make it comfortable for people. And the idea of having a video village at Red Rock at the base of the cliff is really extreme, but they were there doing it. They had their monitors set up, they had their umbrellas out, everybody had their directors chairs. It was pretty classic, but it worked.\n\nCole Heilborn (13:07): Very cool. Well, we're talking about the subject of niche and positioning and focus and expertise. I'd love for you to describe the position and the niche that you have built. I don't know, for yourself or for louder than 11.", "token_estimate": 231, "speakers": ["Jon Glassberg", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "12:26", "end_timestamp": "13:07", "podcast_index": 94, "episode_name": "Ep 94: Finding Your Focus As A Freelancer | Jon Glassberg | Louder Than 11", "slug": "ep-94-finding-your-focus-as-a-freelancer-jon-glassberg-louder-than-11", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-94-finding-your-focus-as-a-freelancer-jon-glassberg-louder-than-11", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Freelancer Month! This whole month, we'll be sharing episodes that are geared more towards freelancers and answering the top questions they may have.", "podcast_length": "44:116"}, {"id": "85613ecc-f727-4cdb-9d87-06a16e5ffaf0", "episode_id": "ffbbb1e5-59f1-4a24-84d5-2d9d6d31b3e1", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4886", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Jon Glassberg (13:20): Yeah, totally. I mean, when we started chatting about this idea, I started thinking about it more and more. And I mean, I think everybody has a unique position that they come to the table with. For me, I am probably in a good way and came to the table with of strong background in rock climbing, and I just had all these connections with climbers, and a lot of those climbers are now 30 and 40 somethings that are in positions at businesses that I work for. So it just kind of became a big networking, long-term lifetime networking thing that kind of paid off in the long run, which is pretty special right place, right time for sure. And being a pro climber and understanding that world and being able to hang with those athletes and also speak that language and be able to know what's cool and what's happening now in the moment kind of set me up to have a pretty cool niche in that space. (14:28): I realized early on as we started making videos about just us and our friends climbing that we're the only ones doing it for free online. There were a few other companies that were providing DVDs and downloads that were paid for in some way, or maybe it was a film festival, kind of like the Real Rock Film festival, but nobody was cranking out the type of media that we on such a consistent level, that was all for free, and that was just expected to get people psyched. We just wanted exposure. We just wanted people to know what we were doing. And I mean, there was maybe one or two other individuals out there that were working on that kind of level. They were pumping out a few things here and there with their climbers that they were associated with, but we were definitely doing probably the biggest and highest volume. (15:18): And it was just, I don't know, it was kind of cool. We were risk takers and we were willing to make terrible videos if it meant that people would talk about it. And we weren't in the business of making commercials yet, or commercial content or brand campaigns or whatever you want to call them these days. But we were just in the business of making things that we thought were cool and documenting our climbing lives. And that niche just carved itself out. I mean, now everybody has a cell phone that has a video camera that can record and upload directly to their channel, and it takes as long as it takes to shoot the video to upload it, and it's online. So I mean, we've come a long way since the early two thousands, but I mean, I still think that the DSLR world and the pre cell phone video world was pretty exciting. (16:15): We were just experimenting. I remember when Vimeo and YouTube were happening, and suddenly there were platforms to put these all on instead of, we used to upload videos to the backend of our website, and we used this player that was made by some guy and it was super buggy and half the people couldn't get it to work. And just all this crazy stuff that now we take for granted. You just click once and your videos uploaded to a site where millions of people can watch it anytime. So it's cool how far it's come, but being on the front line was super fun because we learned so much from just messing up and figuring out what looked good and what looked bad and how to export, and compression settings and color correction. I mean, we had no idea what we were doing. And then over time, we learned, and I took classes and stuff online about how to color correct and premiere because I had no idea how to do that in the early two thousands.", "token_estimate": 883, "speakers": ["Jon Glassberg"], "start_timestamp": "13:20", "end_timestamp": "13:20", "podcast_index": 94, "episode_name": "Ep 94: Finding Your Focus As A Freelancer | Jon Glassberg | Louder Than 11", "slug": "ep-94-finding-your-focus-as-a-freelancer-jon-glassberg-louder-than-11", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-94-finding-your-focus-as-a-freelancer-jon-glassberg-louder-than-11", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Freelancer Month! This whole month, we'll be sharing episodes that are geared more towards freelancers and answering the top questions they may have.", "podcast_length": "44:116"}, {"id": "9fee09f1-f378-4a6d-a4a5-310e4d642f5d", "episode_id": "ffbbb1e5-59f1-4a24-84d5-2d9d6d31b3e1", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4886", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Cole Heilborn (17:13): Yeah, you're like, what do these three color wheels do?\n\nJon Glassberg (17:16): Yeah, yeah, I mean, I knew a lot from room photography days and print world stuff because a lot of the stuff that I did in college was layout and design, and there was color theory and stuff like that, but it was all super archaic. And as right around that time, print was really taking a hit from digital and stuff became more and more online based. And so I just started focusing more on what the web had to offer, and it just exploded. It should have, I mean, it was totally the way forward. And so a lot of that stuff carried through to video, like designing graphics and kind of designing the way that you're presenting the videos that you make and how you're publicizing them and promoting them and stuff like that. So I mean, it all kind of is one and the same if you have a little bit of a graphic design layout mind, you kind of understand a general eye for photography and a general eye for how something will look when you're presenting it and stuff like that. So it goes a long way.\n\nCole Heilborn (18:24): So obviously I know your experience is in the film and photo world, but I think this conversation applies to kind of creative field as a whole, whether that's journalism, writing, graphic design, et cetera. Why is having a niche so important?", "token_estimate": 341, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Jon Glassberg"], "start_timestamp": "17:13", "end_timestamp": "18:24", "podcast_index": 94, "episode_name": "Ep 94: Finding Your Focus As A Freelancer | Jon Glassberg | Louder Than 11", "slug": "ep-94-finding-your-focus-as-a-freelancer-jon-glassberg-louder-than-11", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-94-finding-your-focus-as-a-freelancer-jon-glassberg-louder-than-11", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Freelancer Month! This whole month, we'll be sharing episodes that are geared more towards freelancers and answering the top questions they may have.", "podcast_length": "44:116"}, {"id": "94fbe073-9536-42fc-bd23-a5a99244d90e", "episode_id": "ffbbb1e5-59f1-4a24-84d5-2d9d6d31b3e1", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4886", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Jon Glassberg (18:40): Well, I think as you start out as a freelancer, you're doing everything yourself because you want to save money and you want to make it affordable to get paid to do the work that you do. You don't want to pay for a writer or a graphic designer or an editor or whoever it might be. You kind of do it all yourself and that way you make the most from the project. And maybe you learn a bit about all the different little elements that go into creating something, whether it's a article or a magazine or a film or really good photography or whatever it might be. All of those things have so many layers to pre-production, production and post-production that go into creating something decent. And you start to realize that as a freelancer that you can't just do it all yourself eventually. You have to focus on what you're good at and really kind of dive into that spreading yourself thin. (19:41): You'll be average at all those things all at once when you're trying to pull it off. But if you can focus, you can be really, really good at one specific thing, and that's when you become really valuable in my opinion. And I've kind of realized that over the years. I mean, I've done everything from video editing to producing, to shooting, to assisting literally everything in the photo and video world. And I'm realizing that if I focus and really put my energy all into one of those things, then I can really become good at it and hone my skills. And I think that's slowly becoming directing more and more. And I think directing is also more of a, I think of it also as just kind of a general business manager as well. You might be directing one film and you might be managing that whole production, but I also own a film and photo production company, so there's lots of management that happens there too, and directing of that company. (20:42): So I mean, I think just having that big overarching view on what's happening, whether it's one production or a whole company, you're still directing. So I've slowly become more and more of a director minded person realizing that I can hire really good freelancers that are experts at photography or really good assistants or amazing at lighting, or really good at sound or good editors or whatever it may be that will help elevate a production. But it is something that if I did on my own and I was trying to fill every role, I would be spread super thin and I would just do everything mediocre. And this is a good way to create better products. But I think when you first start, yeah, you're doing it all. So it's really hard to figure out what is your niche, I guess, but also identifying maybe what you have special that's unique to you that no one else really has or maybe other people have, but you're able to really capitalize on it. I think there are plenty of people in the climbing space, adventure expedition, outdoor space that would probably make great film directors and make amazing films, but some people just don't have the mindset or don't have the work ethic or something and they just don't do it. But some do. And when you identify that, then you realize that you have something special.", "token_estimate": 794, "speakers": ["Jon Glassberg"], "start_timestamp": "18:40", "end_timestamp": "18:40", "podcast_index": 94, "episode_name": "Ep 94: Finding Your Focus As A Freelancer | Jon Glassberg | Louder Than 11", "slug": "ep-94-finding-your-focus-as-a-freelancer-jon-glassberg-louder-than-11", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-94-finding-your-focus-as-a-freelancer-jon-glassberg-louder-than-11", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Freelancer Month! This whole month, we'll be sharing episodes that are geared more towards freelancers and answering the top questions they may have.", "podcast_length": "44:116"}, {"id": "f31e6c53-e070-4c80-a0e3-c5c053aa1313", "episode_id": "ffbbb1e5-59f1-4a24-84d5-2d9d6d31b3e1", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4886", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Cole Heilborn (22:17): Let's define a niche. What is a niche to you? How do you know when you are in a niche?\n\nJon Glassberg (22:25): I don't know if you would know it right away. I think you realize it over time once you realize that people are coming to you for your specific type of service or product that you're putting out there. But I think you could probably could have some insight into what's possible for whatever your background is and whatever you're psyched on and whatever you are doing. A lot of that people seem to see value in. I mean, for a long time I was mostly just an editor. I would just edit video constantly, and that was the niche at the time, and I got a lot of requests to do editing, so I just did a ton of editing, and that was awesome because I got a ton of experience in that world, but that wasn't what I wanted to do. So applying that skillset to future work was super valuable.\n\nCole Heilborn (23:24): So you talked about the fact that when you got started, you did everything, and I think a lot of people do everything. That's kind of how I got started. You just jump in and you're like, I want to learn about sound. I want to learn about sound design. I want everything under the sun. And as you get into it, you start to realize there's things that you enjoy and there's things that you don't enjoy. There's things you're really just not good at. I mean, how long did it take for you to do everything and then slowly start to hone yourself into being a director?", "token_estimate": 376, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Jon Glassberg"], "start_timestamp": "22:17", "end_timestamp": "23:24", "podcast_index": 94, "episode_name": "Ep 94: Finding Your Focus As A Freelancer | Jon Glassberg | Louder Than 11", "slug": "ep-94-finding-your-focus-as-a-freelancer-jon-glassberg-louder-than-11", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-94-finding-your-focus-as-a-freelancer-jon-glassberg-louder-than-11", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Freelancer Month! This whole month, we'll be sharing episodes that are geared more towards freelancers and answering the top questions they may have.", "podcast_length": "44:116"}, {"id": "31ceed3a-1554-4e69-867b-9f5abc211c8b", "episode_id": "ffbbb1e5-59f1-4a24-84d5-2d9d6d31b3e1", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4886", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Jon Glassberg (23:53): I mean, first of all, I have a whole lifetime ahead of me of honing skills further. I feel like I could keep refining and get better and better every day. I mean it, it's a lifetime pursuit, but I think I kind of decided to get better at some specific things. I decided that I was sick of being bad at color correction or something, and I was just like, I hate every thing that I try to color correct. It never looks like it matches. No matter how hard I try, it's just not looking good, and I know I can do better. So I just took some classes online and I educated myself, and I'm definitely not a colorist as a profession, but I'm way better than I was and I'm not as embarrassed of it now. So I mean, I think you kind of decide what you need to invest your time into. (24:47): I think your time is precious, and there's so many different directions you could go in to hone different skills, but for me, I feel like it's going to take a long time before I feel comfortable in any space. And the space evolves all the time. So I might get really good at shooting video with the newest camera that I'm using, and then a year later there's a brand new camera with crazy functionality that you're just like, oh my God, this is so much better, but I still need to relearn it or whatever. And same would go for any technology, honestly. It is changing so fast that you're just relearning stuff. It's not always made easier. Sometimes it is more complicated, but it's more complicated, it's better and it just works better.\n\nCole Heilborn (25:32): So from the perspective of finding work and getting hired to do projects, I know that's feedback that we've received. A lot of folks have questions about how do I find work as a freelancer and whatever their discipline is, does a niche play a role in that? If so, how?", "token_estimate": 459, "speakers": ["Jon Glassberg", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "23:53", "end_timestamp": "25:32", "podcast_index": 94, "episode_name": "Ep 94: Finding Your Focus As A Freelancer | Jon Glassberg | Louder Than 11", "slug": "ep-94-finding-your-focus-as-a-freelancer-jon-glassberg-louder-than-11", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-94-finding-your-focus-as-a-freelancer-jon-glassberg-louder-than-11", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Freelancer Month! This whole month, we'll be sharing episodes that are geared more towards freelancers and answering the top questions they may have.", "podcast_length": "44:116"}, {"id": "a2e829b0-0807-4f87-9eb7-28ad50ca5a0e", "episode_id": "ffbbb1e5-59f1-4a24-84d5-2d9d6d31b3e1", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4886", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Jon Glassberg (25:48): Yeah, I mean, I would say going back to what my niche might be is just my work ethic in general. I bust my ass really hard all the time. I'm always working, I'm always thinking about work, and I'm always imagining how to be what would inspire me, but also what would pay the bills. And I guess I think that you, you know what? I don't really know what the right answer to that is. I mean, I think that you find what you're interested in and you pay your dues and you put out as much as you can in, let's use film as an example. If you're interested in outdoor photo and video, you need to be cranking out photos and video projects all the time and not just so that you can get better at your craft so that people just see that you're stuff. (26:45): I know a ton of people that talk about making film projects and talk about how cool this would be if they did that, or they're critical of the outdoor space and why people aren't doing more of this or whatever. And honestly, just go out there and make it. I know that it's hard because you have to have some money to make some projects and you have to kind of be motivated, but I think you can also produce stuff pretty inexpensively that is super inspiring and cool. And people recognize when you put effort towards making stuff, and then that material becomes marketing for your brand down the road. And if you're into film and photo in the outdoor space, people need to know that you're making tons of stuff. Just because you are holding a camera and out there in nature doesn't mean that you're making things. (27:34): People need to actually see the final product. And I often see people with their photo of them on Instagram or Facebook holding a camera and saying that they're a filmmaker or a photographer, but I've never seen anything that they put out, or at least the stuff that they're putting out is not being seen. And I think that that's easy to do with quantity and aligning with people and building relationships with climbers and outdoor enthusiasts that are doing stuff that's interesting. If you make enough stuff, people will see it and will act as its own advertising.", "token_estimate": 541, "speakers": ["Jon Glassberg"], "start_timestamp": "25:48", "end_timestamp": "25:48", "podcast_index": 94, "episode_name": "Ep 94: Finding Your Focus As A Freelancer | Jon Glassberg | Louder Than 11", "slug": "ep-94-finding-your-focus-as-a-freelancer-jon-glassberg-louder-than-11", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-94-finding-your-focus-as-a-freelancer-jon-glassberg-louder-than-11", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Freelancer Month! This whole month, we'll be sharing episodes that are geared more towards freelancers and answering the top questions they may have.", "podcast_length": "44:116"}, {"id": "2e691215-b76b-4782-b8f9-fe11d457503f", "episode_id": "ffbbb1e5-59f1-4a24-84d5-2d9d6d31b3e1", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4886", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Cole Heilborn (28:11): Yeah, no, well said. Yeah, it's kind of a blessing and a curse where you're in the media world, you can produce all of your own content, but that doesn't necessarily mean that it's easier to do. So sometimes it's harder.\n\nJon Glassberg (28:20): I mean, you can make your own YouTube channel. Anybody can make a channel with all of their favorite stuff. And if maybe you've only made a few things, at least you've made a few things and they're online and you can reference them. I mean, I can't tell you how many people reach out to me weekly in email and just say, Hey, I'm interested in the outdoor space and I really love photo and video, and I'm a climber and I want to get involved. Can I assist? Or are you hiring or do you do internships or whatever. And 90% of the time, there's no resume attached, there's no website. I'm just like, who are you? I don't know what you're even doing. At least give me something to click through to look at to see if you're actually making things. That goes a long way.\n\nCole Heilborn (29:03): Yeah. There's this book called The Business of Expertise. Have you read that by chance or heard of it?\n\nJon Glassberg (29:09): No, I have not read it. I've heard of it, but I've not read it.", "token_estimate": 312, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Jon Glassberg"], "start_timestamp": "28:11", "end_timestamp": "29:09", "podcast_index": 94, "episode_name": "Ep 94: Finding Your Focus As A Freelancer | Jon Glassberg | Louder Than 11", "slug": "ep-94-finding-your-focus-as-a-freelancer-jon-glassberg-louder-than-11", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-94-finding-your-focus-as-a-freelancer-jon-glassberg-louder-than-11", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Freelancer Month! This whole month, we'll be sharing episodes that are geared more towards freelancers and answering the top questions they may have.", "podcast_length": "44:116"}]
